window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["fine-tuning-llm"]=[{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0001","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-001-2-1-ethical-considerations","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s003","q":"How can bias in an LLM's training data show up in its behaviour, and what do you do about it?","a":"<p>The model learns statistical patterns from its data, so stereotypes and under-representation in the data appear in its outputs (for instance, associating certain jobs with one gender). Mitigation happens at several points: curate and balance the data, audit it with bias and toxicity detectors, test the trained model on counterfactual prompts (swap a name or gender and compare outputs), and keep monitoring after deployment. Bias cannot be removed completely, so the goal is to measure it and keep it within agreed limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0002","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-001-2-1-ethical-considerations","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s003","q":"A team scores training texts with a Hugging Face toxicity classifier to look for bias. What does that check tell you, and what does it miss?","a":"<p>The pipeline loads a classifier trained to detect toxic language and returns a label and score for each text. It finds abusive or hateful content. It does not measure representational bias such as stereotypes in neutral-sounding sentences, or unequal quality across groups. For that you need counterfactual or demographic tests, fairness metrics (for instance comparing error rates per group), and human review.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from transformers import pipeline\n\nbias_detector = pipeline(&quot;text-classification&quot;, model=&quot;unitary/toxic-bert&quot;)\nresult = bias_detector(&quot;The nurse was very caring and gentle&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0003","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-001-2-1-ethical-considerations","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s003","q":"When explaining a text classifier with input gradients, why does making <code>input_ids</code> require gradients fail, and what do you differentiate instead?","a":"<p><code>input_ids</code> are integer token indices, and PyTorch only allows gradients on floating-point tensors, so this raises an error. Even if it worked, a lookup by integer index is not differentiable. Take the gradient with respect to the embedding vectors instead: compute embeddings with <code>model.get_input_embeddings()</code>, pass them as <code>inputs_embeds</code>, and call <code>backward()</code> on the target logit. Methods such as integrated gradients (Captum) build on this idea.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inputs = tokenizer(&quot;This movie was great&quot;, return_tensors=&quot;pt&quot;)\ninputs[&quot;input_ids&quot;].requires_grad_(True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0004","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-001-2-1-ethical-considerations","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s003","q":"Which items belong on a responsible-AI checklist before an LLM goes to production?","a":"<p>Data: audit for bias, check that groups are represented, remove personal data, record where the data came from. Transparency: model documentation (a model card), explanations of decisions, regular bias tests. Deployment: monitor behaviour across user groups, collect feedback, schedule retraining, state the known limits to users. Governance: an ethics or review board, compliance with regulation, and named owners.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0005","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-001-2-1-ethical-considerations","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s003","q":"Why must personal data (PII) be removed from LLM training data?","a":"<p>Large models can memorise rare training strings and reproduce them when prompted, which leaks emails, phone numbers or records. Removal also keeps you within privacy law (GDPR, for instance). Use detection and redaction before training, deduplicate the data (duplicates are memorised most), and test the trained model for extraction.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0006","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-001-2-1-ethical-considerations","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s003","q":"What is a model card, and why does it matter for transparency?","a":"<p>A model card is a short document that states what a model is for, what data it was trained on, how it was evaluated (including per-group results), and its known limits and risks. It lets users and auditors decide whether the model fits their use and where it may fail, which is hard to do from the weights alone.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0007","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-001-2-1-ethical-considerations","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s003","q":"Why monitor an LLM across demographic groups after launch and not only before?","a":"<p>Real user traffic differs from the test set, and a model that looked fair on a benchmark can fail for groups that were rare in it. Feedback loops can also amplify bias: if model outputs shape future data, skew grows. Monitoring per group catches this early and tells you when to retrain or add guardrails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0008","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-002-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"Compare encoder-only, decoder-only and encoder-decoder transformers: how each is trained and what each is used for.","a":"<p>Encoder-only (BERT) reads the whole input in both directions and is trained with masked language modelling, so it suits understanding tasks such as classification, sentiment and extractive question answering. Decoder-only (GPT) predicts the next token left to right (autoregressive objective), so it suits open-ended generation and is the base of most chat models. Encoder-decoder (T5, FLAN-T5) encodes the input and then generates an output conditioned on it, so it suits translation, summarisation and other input-to-output tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0009","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-002-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"Why does a decoder-only model attend only to earlier tokens, while BERT attends to tokens on both sides?","a":"<p>A decoder-only model is trained to predict the next token, so each position must not see the future token it is asked to predict. A causal attention mask enforces this. BERT predicts masked tokens from the surrounding context, so seeing both sides is allowed and gives richer representations for understanding, but it cannot generate text token by token in the same natural way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0010","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-002-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"What is the difference between the masked language modelling objective and the autoregressive objective?","a":"<p>Masked language modelling hides some input tokens (about 15 percent in BERT) and trains the model to recover them from both sides. Autoregressive modelling trains the model to predict each token from only the tokens before it. The first yields strong encoders for classification. The second yields models that can continue text, which is what generation needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0011","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-002-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"You must classify millions of product reviews per day as positive or negative at low cost. Which architecture and model size would you pick, and why?","a":"<p>An encoder-only model such as BERT, or a smaller distilled one such as DistilBERT, with a classification head fine-tuned on labelled reviews. It is cheap to run, fast, and accurate for this narrow task. A large decoder-only LLM would cost far more per request and add latency for no gain. Use an LLM with prompting only when labelled data is missing or the task changes often.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0012","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-002-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"In this BERT snippet, what does <code>num_labels=2</code> do, and why does the library print a warning when the model loads?","a":"<p><code>num_labels=2</code> adds a classification layer with two outputs on top of the pre-trained BERT body. That layer is newly and randomly initialised, so the library warns that some weights were not loaded from the checkpoint and that you should train the model before using it. Predictions are meaningless until it is fine-tuned.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from transformers import BertForSequenceClassification\n\nmodel = BertForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained(&#x27;bert-base-uncased&#x27;, \n                                                     num_labels=2)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0013","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-002-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"Why does the sentiment function wrap inference in <code>torch.no_grad()</code> and apply softmax to <code>outputs.logits</code>?","a":"<p><code>torch.no_grad()</code> turns off gradient tracking, which saves memory and time because inference does not need backpropagation. The model returns raw logits, which are unbounded scores. Softmax over the last dimension turns them into probabilities that sum to 1 across the classes.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with torch.no_grad():\n    outputs = model(**inputs)\n    predictions = torch.nn.functional.softmax(outputs.logits, dim=-1)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0014","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-002-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"Why does BERT's tokenizer call use <code>max_length=512</code> with <code>truncation=True</code>?","a":"<p>BERT was pre-trained with learned absolute position embeddings for 512 positions, so it cannot take longer inputs. <code>truncation=True</code> cuts anything beyond <code>max_length</code> instead of raising an error, and <code>padding=True</code> pads the shorter sequences in a batch to the same length. For long documents, split into chunks or use a long-context model.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inputs = tokenizer(text, return_tensors=&quot;pt&quot;, \n                  max_length=512, truncation=True, padding=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0015","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-002-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"Why does GPT-2 code often set <code>tokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token</code>?","a":"<p>GPT-2 was trained without a padding token, so its tokenizer has none, and padding a batch fails. Reusing the end-of-sequence token as padding is the usual workaround. Attention masks tell the model to ignore those positions. For batched generation with decoder-only models, pad on the left so new tokens follow the real text.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tokenizer = GPT2Tokenizer.from_pretrained(&#x27;gpt2&#x27;)\ntokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0016","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-002-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"In Hugging Face, what happens to <code>temperature=0.7</code> in this call, and how many tokens does <code>max_length</code> count for a decoder-only model?","a":"<p>By default <code>generate</code> uses greedy decoding, so <code>temperature</code> has no effect unless <code>do_sample=True</code> is set (recent transformers versions warn about this). For a decoder-only model <code>max_length</code> counts the prompt tokens plus the new tokens, so a long prompt leaves little room; <code>max_new_tokens</code> limits only the newly produced tokens and is usually clearer.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>outputs = model.generate(\n    inputs,\n    max_length=100,\n    num_return_sequences=1,\n    temperature=0.7,\n    pad_token_id=tokenizer.eos_token_id\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0017","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-003-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"What does \"text-to-text\" mean for T5, and how does the model know which task to do?","a":"<p>Every task is cast as taking a text string and producing a text string, including classification and translation. A task prefix at the start of the input names the task, for instance <code>translate English to German: ...</code> or <code>summarize: ...</code>. The same model, loss and decoding procedure then serve all tasks, so no task-specific heads are needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0018","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-003-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"Why is an encoder-decoder a good fit for translation and summarisation?","a":"<p>The encoder reads the whole source text with bidirectional attention and builds a representation of it. The decoder produces the output token by token and uses cross-attention to look back at the encoder output. That separation suits tasks where the output is a transformed version of a given input. Decoder-only models can do these tasks too, with a prompt, but the input and output share one sequence.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0019","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-003-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"What was T5's pre-training objective?","a":"<p>T5 used span corruption: random spans of the input text are replaced by sentinel tokens, and the decoder must output the missing spans. It was then trained on a mixture of supervised tasks in text-to-text form. Spans make the target shorter than the input, which also makes training cheaper than reconstructing the whole text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0020","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-003-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"The original <code>t5-small</code> can translate English to German or French with a task prefix, but not to Spanish. Why?","a":"<p>Its supervised training mixture included only specific translation pairs (English to German, French and Romanian). A prefix only triggers behaviour the model has seen, so unseen language pairs fail or produce poor text. FLAN-T5 is instruction-tuned on a much wider set of tasks and handles more pairs, and you can fine-tune for a new pair.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0021","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-003-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"What does the <code>num_beams=4, early_stopping=True</code> pair do in T5's <code>generate</code> call?","a":"<p><code>num_beams=4</code> runs beam search: it keeps the 4 most probable partial outputs at each step instead of only the single best token (greedy). That finds higher-probability sequences at a cost in time and memory that grows with the beam count. <code>early_stopping=True</code> ends the search as soon as enough beams have finished, instead of searching until <code>max_length</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>outputs = model.generate(\n    inputs,\n    max_length=150,\n    num_beams=4,\n    early_stopping=True\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0022","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-003-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"What is the practical difference between <code>T5ForConditionalGeneration</code> and an <code>AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM</code> class?","a":"<p><code>T5ForConditionalGeneration</code> is the concrete class for T5 models: encoder, decoder and language-model head. <code>AutoModelForSeq2SeqLM</code> reads the checkpoint's config and returns the right concrete class, so the same code works for T5, BART, FLAN-T5 and others. Use the Auto class for portable code, and the concrete class when you need model-specific options.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0023","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-003-2-2-architecture-types-interview-staple","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s004","q":"Why might you pick an encoder-decoder such as T5 or BART over a decoder-only model for summarising long documents?","a":"<p>The encoder reads the whole input once, bidirectionally, and the decoder only generates the short summary, with cross-attention to the encoder output. Compute is spent on understanding the input, and the output length is small. A decoder-only model must keep the whole input plus the output in one growing sequence and attends causally, so it needs a long context window and the cost grows with both. Decoder-only models are still a good choice when you want one general model for many tasks through prompting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0024","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-004-2-3-model-selection-by-task-and-budget","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s005","q":"How would you choose a model for a new NLP task when you have a limited compute budget?","a":"<p>Start from the task type. Understanding tasks (classification, extraction) fit encoder-only models, generation fits decoder-only models, input-to-output tasks fit encoder-decoder models. Then take the smallest model that meets the quality bar: build a baseline with a small or distilled model, measure it on your own data, and scale up only if the gap justifies the cost. Check latency, memory, licence and the amount of labelled data available as well.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0025","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-004-2-3-model-selection-by-task-and-budget","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s005","q":"Why is DistilBERT a common low-budget pick compared with BERT-base?","a":"<p>DistilBERT is trained with knowledge distillation, where a small student learns to mimic a larger teacher. It is about 40 percent smaller and 60 percent faster than BERT-base while keeping roughly 97 percent of its language-understanding performance (figures from the DistilBERT paper). That makes it a good default when latency or hardware is limited.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0026","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-004-2-3-model-selection-by-task-and-budget","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s005","q":"What is knowledge distillation, and when would you use it?","a":"<p>A small student model is trained to match the output distribution (soft labels) of a large teacher, instead of only the hard labels. The student learns the teacher's behaviour in a smaller footprint. Use it when you need the teacher's quality at lower cost, for instance on mobile, in edge devices or in high-traffic services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0027","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-004-2-3-model-selection-by-task-and-budget","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s005","q":"When would you prompt a large hosted LLM instead of fine-tuning a small model?","a":"<p>When there is little or no labelled data, the task changes often, or you need to ship quickly. Prompting has no training step and one model serves many tasks. Fine-tuning a small model wins when you have labelled data, a stable narrow task, strict latency or cost limits, or data that cannot leave your environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0028","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-004-2-3-model-selection-by-task-and-budget","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s005","q":"In this lookup, why is the first default <code>{}</code> rather than <code>None</code>?","a":"<p>If <code>task_type</code> is unknown, the first <code>.get</code> must return something that still has a <code>.get</code> method. An empty dict makes the second <code>.get</code> return the fallback string. With <code>None</code> the second call would raise <code>AttributeError</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>return recommendations.get(task_type, {}).get(computational_budget, &#x27;Consider task-specific fine-tuning&#x27;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0029","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-005-2-4-flan-t5-in-focus","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s006","q":"What is instruction tuning, and how is FLAN-T5 different from the original T5?","a":"<p>Instruction tuning continues training a pre-trained model on a large collection of tasks written as natural-language instructions with their answers. FLAN-T5 is T5 fine-tuned this way (the Flan collection, over a thousand tasks, some with chain-of-thought data). The result follows instructions it was never trained on, works zero-shot without rigid task prefixes, and benefits more from few-shot examples than the base T5.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0030","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-005-2-4-flan-t5-in-focus","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s006","q":"Why does an instruction-tuned model do better zero-shot than a plain pre-trained model?","a":"<p>A plain pre-trained model only learned to continue or fill in text, so a request is just text to continue. Instruction tuning teaches it the pattern \"instruction in, helpful answer out\" across many tasks, and that behaviour generalises to new instructions. It is the step that turns a language model into something that follows requests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0031","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-005-2-4-flan-t5-in-focus","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s006","q":"How do you estimate the memory needed to run a model for inference?","a":"<p>Multiply parameters by bytes per parameter: 4 for float32, 2 for float16 or bfloat16, 1 for int8, about 0.5 for 4-bit. A 3-billion-parameter model needs about 12 GB in float32 or 6 GB in float16 for the weights alone. Add overhead for activations, the key-value cache and batch size; a 1.2 to 1.5 factor is a rough rule, and long contexts or large batches need more.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0032","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-005-2-4-flan-t5-in-focus","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s006","q":"Why does fine-tuning a model need much more memory than running it?","a":"<p>Training stores gradients (same size as the weights) and optimiser state. Adam keeps two extra values per parameter. In float32 that is roughly 16 bytes per trainable parameter for weights, gradients and Adam states, before activations. Freezing most weights, using parameter-efficient methods, lower precision, gradient checkpointing, or a smaller batch reduces this.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0033","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-005-2-4-flan-t5-in-focus","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s006","q":"How do you pick between <code>flan-t5-base</code>, <code>large</code> and <code>xl</code> for a project?","a":"<p>Larger models are generally more accurate and follow harder instructions better, but need more GPU memory, run slower and cost more. Prototype with the smallest size that fits your hardware, evaluate on your own task, and move up only while the quality gain justifies the cost. Fine-tuning a smaller model on task data often beats prompting a larger one for a narrow task.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0034","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-006-2-4-flan-t5-in-focus","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s006","q":"In this wrapper, what happens when both <code>num_beams=5</code> and <code>do_sample=True</code> are set, and why mix them rarely?","a":"<p>In Hugging Face this runs beam-search multinomial sampling: several beams are kept and tokens are sampled instead of taking the top ones. It is allowed, but beam search aims for the most probable sequence while sampling aims for variety, so the two goals pull against each other. Pick one: beams for deterministic, precise output (translation, summarisation) or sampling for varied output (creative writing).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>outputs = self.model.generate(\n    inputs[&quot;input_ids&quot;],\n    max_length=max_length,\n    num_beams=5,\n    early_stopping=True,\n    temperature=temperature,\n    do_sample=True if temperature &gt; 0 else False\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0035","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-006-2-4-flan-t5-in-focus","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s006","q":"What does <code>temperature</code> do during generation, and why can't you pass <code>temperature=0</code> together with <code>do_sample=True</code>?","a":"<p>Temperature divides the logits before softmax. A low value sharpens the distribution toward the most likely token, and a high value flattens it so rarer tokens appear more. A value of 0 would divide by zero, and Hugging Face rejects it. For deterministic output use <code>do_sample=False</code> (greedy or beam search) instead of a zero temperature.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0036","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-006-2-4-flan-t5-in-focus","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s006","q":"What does <code>skip_special_tokens=True</code> do when decoding model output?","a":"<p>It removes tokens such as <code>&lt;pad&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;/s&gt;</code> (end of sequence) from the decoded text. Without it you see these markers in the output, which breaks downstream string handling and metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0037","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-006-2-4-flan-t5-in-focus","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s006","q":"Why create the model and tokenizer once in <code>__init__</code> of a wrapper class instead of inside each call?","a":"<p>Loading a model reads gigabytes from disk and moves them to the device, which takes seconds to minutes. Doing it per request makes every call slow and can exhaust memory. Load once, reuse the object, and keep generation settings as arguments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0038","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-006-2-4-flan-t5-in-focus","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s006","q":"What is the difference between sampling with <code>top_k</code>, <code>top_p</code> and temperature?","a":"<p>Temperature rescales probabilities. <code>top_k</code> keeps only the k most likely tokens and samples among them. <code>top_p</code> (nucleus sampling) keeps the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add to p, so the candidate set size adapts to how confident the model is. They are often combined: a moderate temperature with <code>top_p</code> around 0.9 is a common choice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0039","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-007-3-1-what-a-prompt-is-and-how-you-talk-to","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s007","q":"What is a prompt, and what makes a good one?","a":"<p>A prompt is the instruction plus any context and examples that you give an LLM, and it determines the response. A good prompt is clear, specific, concise, and gives context: the task, the audience or role, the input data, constraints, and the output format you want.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0040","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-007-3-1-what-a-prompt-is-and-how-you-talk-to","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s007","q":"What are the ways to use an LLM, and how do you choose between a web UI and the API?","a":"<p>You can use a hosted application or web interface with no code, or call a model from code through an API or a local library. A UI suits exploration and one-off work. An API or library is needed for automation, integration into products, batch jobs, testing prompts at scale and controlling parameters such as temperature and maximum length.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0041","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-007-3-1-what-a-prompt-is-and-how-you-talk-to","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s007","q":"Where should the API key for an LLM provider live in this client code?","a":"<p>Not in the source code. Read it from an environment variable or a secrets manager (the OpenAI client reads <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code> automatically), keep it out of version control, and use per-environment keys that can be rotated and restricted. A key committed to a repository should be treated as leaked and revoked.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import openai\n\nclient = openai.OpenAI(api_key=&quot;your-api-key&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0042","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-007-3-1-what-a-prompt-is-and-how-you-talk-to","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s007","q":"In a chat completion call, what are the message roles for, and why does the call use a list?","a":"<p>Each message has a role: <code>system</code> sets behaviour and rules, <code>user</code> is the person's input, and <code>assistant</code> is the model's earlier replies. The API is stateless, so the list carries the whole conversation each time, and the model sees the history in that order. Few-shot examples can be given as earlier user and assistant turns.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=&quot;gpt-3.5-turbo&quot;,\n    messages=[{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: &quot;Explain quantum computing&quot;}]\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0043","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-007-3-1-what-a-prompt-is-and-how-you-talk-to","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s007","q":"Why does T5 need a task prefix such as <code>summarize:</code>, while a chat model like GPT can be asked in plain language?","a":"<p>Original T5 was trained on a fixed set of tasks identified by prefixes, so the prefix is how it selects the task. Chat models are instruction-tuned on free-form requests, so plain language works. FLAN-T5 sits in between: it handles natural instructions but still responds well to clear task wording.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0044","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-007-3-1-what-a-prompt-is-and-how-you-talk-to","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s007","q":"In a chat completion API, what do <code>temperature</code> and <code>max_tokens</code> control, and when would you change them?","a":"<p><code>temperature</code> controls randomness: low values (0 to 0.3) give focused, repeatable output for extraction, classification and code, and higher values give more varied output for brainstorming or creative text. <code>max_tokens</code> caps the length of the reply, which bounds cost and latency, but a cap that is too small cuts the answer off mid-sentence (the finish reason shows <code>length</code>). Change one at a time, and set the cap from the longest answer you expect.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0045","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-008-3-2-colab-model-setup","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s008","q":"In <code>TFAutoModelForSeq2SeqLM</code>, what do the <code>TF</code> prefix and the <code>Seq2SeqLM</code> part mean?","a":"<p><code>TF</code> means the TensorFlow version of the class (without it you get PyTorch). <code>Seq2SeqLM</code> means a sequence-to-sequence language model with an output head that generates text, as needed for translation and summarisation. <code>Auto</code> means the class picks the right architecture from the checkpoint's config. TensorFlow support in transformers is being phased out, so new projects usually use the PyTorch classes.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from transformers import TFAutoModelForSeq2SeqLM, AutoTokenizer\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(&quot;google/flan-t5-large&quot;)\nmodel = TFAutoModelForSeq2SeqLM.from_pretrained(&quot;google/flan-t5-large&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0046","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-008-3-2-colab-model-setup","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s008","q":"Why must the tokenizer and the model come from the same checkpoint?","a":"<p>The tokenizer defines the vocabulary and how text becomes ids, and the model's embedding table is indexed by exactly those ids. A different tokenizer maps the same text to different ids, so the model reads nonsense. <code>AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained</code> with the same name guarantees a match.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0047","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-008-3-2-colab-model-setup","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s008","q":"What does <code>from_pretrained</code> do, and where do the weights go?","a":"<p>It downloads the config, tokenizer files and weights from the Hugging Face Hub (or reads a local path), builds the model and loads the weights into it. Files are cached on disk (by default under <code>~/.cache/huggingface</code>), so later calls load from the cache. Large models need enough disk and RAM or GPU memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0048","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-008-3-2-colab-model-setup","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s008","q":"Why use a GPU runtime for LLM work, and what can you still do on a free one?","a":"<p>A GPU does the large matrix multiplications of a transformer in parallel, making inference and training many times faster than a CPU. Free GPU tiers are enough for prototyping with small and mid-size models or short fine-tuning runs, but have limited memory, time limits and no guarantee of availability. Larger models need paid or dedicated hardware, or smaller and quantised models.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0049","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-008-3-2-colab-model-setup","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s008","q":"In a Jupyter notebook, what does <code>!pip install</code> do, and what is the better form?","a":"<p>The <code>!</code> runs a shell command from the notebook. The installed package may go to a different Python environment than the running kernel. <code>%pip install</code> installs into the kernel's own environment, so it is the recommended form, and the kernel may need a restart after installing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0050","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-009-3-3-the-four-steps-every-task-uses-the-s","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s009","q":"What are the four steps to run any task on an encoder-decoder model, and why does each exist?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Define the prompt, including the task prefix. 2) Tokenize it, because the model works on token ids and not text. 3) Call <code>model.generate</code>, which runs autoregressive decoding. 4) Decode the output ids back to text with the tokenizer. Only the prompt and the length limits change between tasks.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0051","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-009-3-3-the-four-steps-every-task-uses-the-s","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s009","q":"What does the tokenizer return, and why is <code>return_tensors</code> needed?","a":"<p>It returns a dictionary-like object with <code>input_ids</code> (token ids) and <code>attention_mask</code> (1 for real tokens, 0 for padding). Without <code>return_tensors</code> you get plain Python lists. <code>\"tf\"</code> returns TensorFlow tensors, <code>\"pt\"</code> returns PyTorch tensors, and the model needs the tensor type of its framework.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inputs = tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors=&quot;tf&quot;, max_length=512, truncation=True, padding=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0052","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-009-3-3-the-four-steps-every-task-uses-the-s","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s009","q":"In this call, what do <code>truncation=True</code> and <code>padding=True</code> change, and what happens to a long prompt?","a":"<p><code>truncation=True</code> cuts tokens beyond <code>max_length</code> (from the end by default), so the end of a long prompt, often the actual question, is lost. <code>padding=True</code> pads the sequences in a batch to the longest one in that batch. <code>padding=\"max_length\"</code> pads everything to <code>max_length</code> instead. A single prompt needs no padding.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inputs = tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors=&quot;tf&quot;, max_length=512, truncation=True, padding=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0053","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-009-3-3-the-four-steps-every-task-uses-the-s","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s009","q":"What is the difference between <code>max_length</code> in the tokenizer call and <code>max_length</code> in <code>generate</code>?","a":"<p>In the tokenizer it caps the input length in tokens. In <code>generate</code> it caps the length of the output sequence. For an encoder-decoder model that is the decoder output, so input and output limits are independent. Newer code prefers <code>max_new_tokens</code> for the output limit because its meaning is clearer.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>outputs = model.generate(inputs[&quot;input_ids&quot;], max_length=150, num_beams=5, early_stopping=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0054","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-009-3-3-the-four-steps-every-task-uses-the-s","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s009","q":"Why do summarisation, translation and question answering use different output <code>max_length</code> values?","a":"<p>The output length differs by task: summaries are medium, a sentence translation is roughly as long as its source, and extractive answers are short. A tight limit saves time, stops rambling and cuts off runaway repetition, while a limit that is too small truncates the answer mid-sentence. Set it from the longest realistic output for the task.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0055","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-009-3-3-the-four-steps-every-task-uses-the-s","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s009","q":"What is the difference between greedy decoding, beam search and sampling, and when do you use each?","a":"<p>Greedy picks the single most likely token at each step: fast, but can miss better sequences. Beam search keeps several candidates and returns the most probable full sequence: better for translation and summarisation where a precise answer is expected, but slower. Sampling draws from the probability distribution (with temperature, top-k or top-p): gives variety, suited to creative text, but is less repeatable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0056","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-009-3-3-the-four-steps-every-task-uses-the-s","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s009","q":"Why is <code>outputs[0]</code> decoded, and not <code>outputs</code>?","a":"<p><code>generate</code> returns a batch of sequences, one row per input (and per returned sequence). With a single prompt the batch size is 1, so <code>outputs[0]</code> is the one answer. <code>tokenizer.decode</code> handles one sequence, while <code>batch_decode</code> handles all rows.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>print(tokenizer.decode(outputs[0], skip_special_tokens=True))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0057","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-009-3-3-the-four-steps-every-task-uses-the-s","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s009","q":"How do you prompt FLAN-T5 for question answering over a passage?","a":"<p>Put the context first and then the question with a marker, as in the snippet, or use the instruction form <code>Answer the question based on the context. Context: ... Question: ...</code>. The answer then comes from the context, which reduces made-up answers. Keep the context within the input token limit.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>context_question = &quot;The Great Wall of China is over 13,000 miles long. question: How long is the Great Wall of China?&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0058","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-010-3-4-reusable-helpers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s010","q":"Why wrap tokenize, generate and decode in a single helper function?","a":"<p>The three steps are the same for every task, so one function removes duplication and keeps settings in one place, such as truncation and beam count. Tasks then differ only in the prompt and <code>max_length</code>. It is also a single place to add logging, retries and validation.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def flan_t5_generate(prompt, max_length=150):\n    inputs = tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors=&quot;tf&quot;, max_length=512, \n                      truncation=True, padding=True)\n    outputs = model.generate(inputs[&quot;input_ids&quot;], max_length=max_length, \n                           num_beams=5, early_stopping=True)\n    return tokenizer.decode(outputs[0], skip_special_tokens=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0059","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-010-3-4-reusable-helpers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s010","q":"In this task router, what does <code>**kwargs</code> do, and what happens for an unknown <code>task_type</code>?","a":"<p><code>**kwargs</code> collects optional keyword arguments such as <code>target</code> or <code>context</code>, so each task can take its own extra inputs. For an unknown <code>task_type</code>, <code>.get</code> falls back to the raw <code>content</code>, so the model gets a prompt with no task instruction and returns something arbitrary without any error. Raising an error for unknown tasks is safer.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def route_task(task_type, content, **kwargs):\n    task_prompts = {\n        &quot;summarize&quot;: f&quot;summarize: {content}&quot;,\n        ...\n    }\n    prompt = task_prompts.get(task_type, content)\n    return flan_t5_generate(prompt)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0060","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-010-3-4-reusable-helpers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s010","q":"How would you translate one text into several languages with one model?","a":"<p>Build one prompt per target language by changing the prefix, then call the model once for each, or put all prompts in one batch so they run in parallel. One multilingual model such as FLAN-T5 covers many pairs. For quality-critical pairs, evaluate each one, because quality varies a lot between languages.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def translate_text(text, source_lang=&quot;English&quot;, target_lang=&quot;French&quot;):\n    prompt = f&quot;translate {source_lang} to {target_lang}: {text}&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0061","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-011-3-4-reusable-helpers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s010","q":"What is the difference between looping over prompts and true batched generation?","a":"<p>A loop makes one <code>generate</code> call per prompt, so the GPU handles one sequence at a time. In batched generation the tokenizer takes a list, pads to a common length, and one <code>generate</code> call processes all prompts together, which uses the GPU's parallelism and gives much higher throughput. The limit is memory, so choose a batch size that fits and sort inputs by length to reduce padding.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inputs = tokenizer(batch, return_tensors=&quot;tf&quot;, padding=True, \n                  truncation=True, max_length=512)\noutputs = model.generate(inputs[&quot;input_ids&quot;], max_length=150, \n                        num_beams=3, early_stopping=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0062","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-011-3-4-reusable-helpers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s010","q":"Why should batched <code>generate</code> also receive the attention mask?","a":"<p>With padding, short prompts contain pad tokens. Passing only <code>input_ids</code> hides which positions are padding, so the model may attend to them and lower the quality. Call <code>model.generate(**inputs, ...)</code> or pass <code>attention_mask=inputs[\"attention_mask\"]</code>. A single unpadded prompt does not show the problem, which is why it goes unnoticed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>outputs = model.generate(inputs[&quot;input_ids&quot;], max_length=150, \n                        num_beams=3, early_stopping=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0063","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-011-3-4-reusable-helpers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s010","q":"What is a simpler way to decode every row of a batch of outputs?","a":"<p><code>tokenizer.batch_decode(outputs, skip_special_tokens=True)</code> returns the list of decoded strings in one call.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for output in outputs:\n    results.append(tokenizer.decode(output, skip_special_tokens=True))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0064","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-011-3-4-reusable-helpers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s010","q":"Can one batch mix different tasks, such as a summary prompt and a translation prompt, with T5 models?","a":"<p>Yes. The task is identified by each prompt's own prefix, so the model handles each row on its own. Rows can have different output lengths: the batch continues until every sequence finishes, with finished ones padded. If lengths differ a lot, group similar tasks together to avoid wasted compute.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0065","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-011-3-4-reusable-helpers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s010","q":"How do you pick the batch size for inference?","a":"<p>Raise it until the GPU memory or latency limit is reached. Larger batches increase throughput but also per-request latency, and memory grows with batch size, sequence length and beam count. Out-of-memory errors are handled by lowering batch size, input length or beams, or by using half precision.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0066","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-012-3-5-robustness-validation-speed","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s011","q":"What can you change to make FLAN-T5 inference faster or lighter in production, and what do you lose?","a":"<p>Use a smaller model, shorter input and output limits, and fewer beams. Each cuts compute and memory but may reduce quality: the model is less accurate, long inputs are truncated, outputs may be cut off, and fewer beams can lower the quality of the best sequence. Other options are quantisation, half precision, batching, caching repeated requests, and optimised serving engines. Measure quality after each change.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inputs = tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors=&quot;tf&quot;, max_length=256, truncation=True)\noutputs = model.generate(\n    inputs[&quot;input_ids&quot;],\n    max_length=100,\n    num_beams=3,\n    early_stopping=True\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0067","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-012-3-5-robustness-validation-speed","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s011","q":"What problems does this retry wrapper have?","a":"<p>It retries immediately with no backoff, so a rate-limited service gets hit again at once. It catches every exception, including permanent ones such as a bad key or a malformed request, which should not be retried. A failure returns an ordinary string that callers can mistake for a real answer, and if the output keeps failing validation it returns <code>None</code> silently. Better: retry only transient errors with exponential backoff and jitter, and raise a clear exception at the end.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def robust_prompt_execution(prompt, max_retries=3):\n    for attempt in range(max_retries):\n        try:\n            response = query_llm(prompt)\n            if validate_response(response):\n                return response\n        except Exception as e:\n            if attempt == max_retries - 1:\n                return f&quot;Error after {max_retries} attempts: {str(e)}&quot;\n            continue\n    return None</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0068","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-012-3-5-robustness-validation-speed","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s011","q":"Why validate LLM output before using it, and what can you check?","a":"<p>Output is non-deterministic and can be empty, truncated, off-format, or wrong. Check structure first (for instance valid JSON that matches a schema), then length limits, required fields, allowed values, language and banned content. Retry or fall back when a check fails. For correctness, add task checks, a reference-based metric, or a second model as a judge.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0069","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-012-3-5-robustness-validation-speed","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s011","q":"Are simple length or keyword checks enough to validate an LLM response?","a":"<p>They catch obvious failures such as empty output or a model echoing the instruction, but they say nothing about correctness or faithfulness. Use them as cheap first-line guards and add real evaluation: metrics against references, a labelled test set, an LLM-as-judge or human review of a sample.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>validators = {\n    &quot;summarize&quot;: lambda x: len(x.split()) &lt; 50,\n    &quot;translate&quot;: lambda x: len(x) &gt; 0 and not x.startswith(&quot;translate&quot;),\n    &quot;question&quot;: lambda x: len(x.split()) &lt; 20\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0070","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-012-3-5-robustness-validation-speed","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s011","q":"What should you do about timeouts and rate limits when calling an LLM API?","a":"<p>Set a client timeout, retry transient failures (timeouts, 429 and 5xx responses) with exponential backoff and jitter, respect <code>Retry-After</code> headers, cap total attempts, and use a circuit breaker or fallback model if the service stays down. Queue or batch requests to stay under rate limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0071","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-013-4-prompt-engineering-patterns","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s012","q":"What practices should you follow when prompting an encoder-decoder model such as FLAN-T5 in production?","a":"<p>Use clear task wording or the prefix the model expects, set the output length per task, keep inputs inside the token limit, validate the outputs, and batch requests for throughput. Test prompt changes on a fixed evaluation set before rolling them out, since small wording changes can alter results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0072","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-014-4-1-core-patterns","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s013","q":"What is a prompt pattern, and why use patterns rather than writing prompts ad hoc?","a":"<p>A prompt pattern is a reusable structure for a recurring problem, like a design pattern in software (few-shot, chain of thought, role, question refinement, and so on). Patterns give repeatable results, are easier to test and share, and let you combine techniques instead of rediscovering them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0073","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-014-4-1-core-patterns","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s013","q":"What is the cognitive verifier pattern, when do you use it, and what does it cost?","a":"<p>The model first asks clarifying sub-questions, then combines all the answers into a final response. Use it when the request is vague or when a good answer needs information the user has not given. It costs extra turns and tokens, so it is wasteful when the question is already specific.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>system_prompt = &quot;&quot;&quot;Whenever I ask a question, only ask me for additional \ninformation to clarify what I&#x27;m asking before providing a final answer. \nPlease combine all my responses.&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0074","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-014-4-1-core-patterns","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s013","q":"What is the question refinement pattern, and how does it differ from the cognitive verifier?","a":"<p>The model rewrites the user's question into a better, more precise one and asks whether to use it. The cognitive verifier collects missing information through several clarifying questions. Refinement fixes how the question is worded, and the verifier fills gaps in what the model knows.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>system_prompt = &quot;&quot;&quot;When I ask a question, suggest a better question \nand ask me if I would like to use it instead.&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0075","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-014-4-1-core-patterns","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s013","q":"How does the gameplay pattern work, and when is it useful?","a":"<p>You ask the model to turn a topic into a game with rules and then play it. It makes practice and learning interactive, for instance quizzes, role-play drills or scenario training. It suits education and training, and has no value where you need a plain direct answer.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def gameplay_prompt(topic):\n    return f&quot;Create a game for me around {topic}. Give fundamental rules of the game.&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0076","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-014-4-1-core-patterns","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s013","q":"What is chain-of-thought prompting, why does it help, and what are its limits?","a":"<p>You ask the model to write its reasoning steps before the final answer. This helps with arithmetic, logic and multi-step questions, because each step conditions the next. Costs: more output tokens and latency. Limits: it helps mainly in larger models (small models may produce plausible but wrong chains), and the written reasoning is not guaranteed to reflect how the model actually reached the answer.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def chain_of_thought_prompt(problem):\n    return f&quot;&quot;&quot;Solve this step by step:\n\n    Problem: {problem}\n\n    Let&#x27;s think through this step by step:\n    1.&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0077","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-014-4-1-core-patterns","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s013","q":"How can you make chain-of-thought answers more reliable?","a":"<p>Sample several reasoning paths at a non-zero temperature and take the majority final answer (self-consistency). You can also add worked examples with reasoning (few-shot chain of thought), check the final answer with code or a calculator for arithmetic, and verify steps with a second pass.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0078","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-014-4-1-core-patterns","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s013","q":"What does role-based prompting do, and what are its limits?","a":"<p>A role such as \"senior Python reviewer\" sets the tone, vocabulary and depth of the answer and what to focus on. It does not add knowledge the model lacks, so it does not make answers more correct by itself, and a role claiming expertise can make errors sound more confident. Combine it with specific instructions and constraints.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def role_based_prompt(role, task):\n    return f&quot;You are a {role}. {task}&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0079","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-014-4-1-core-patterns","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s013","q":"Why does the few-shot prompt builder end with <code>Output:</code> and no answer?","a":"<p>The final <code>Output:</code> leaves the pattern unfinished, so the most likely continuation is the answer in the same format as the examples. Consistent labels and separators between examples teach the format. If the prompt ended after the input, the model might add commentary rather than the answer.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def few_shot_prompt(examples, new_task):\n    prompt = &quot;&quot;\n    for example in examples:\n        prompt += f&quot;Input: {example[&#x27;input&#x27;]}\\nOutput: {example[&#x27;output&#x27;]}\\n\\n&quot;\n    prompt += f&quot;Input: {new_task}\\nOutput:&quot;\n    return prompt</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0080","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-014-4-1-core-patterns","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s013","q":"What is zero-shot chain of thought, and how does it differ from few-shot chain of thought?","a":"<p>Zero-shot chain of thought adds only a trigger such as \"Let's think step by step\" with no worked examples, and the model produces its own reasoning. It is cheap to write and works reasonably on large models. Few-shot chain of thought supplies example problems with reasoning, so the reasoning style and answer format are controlled and usually more reliable, but the examples use more tokens and must be written well.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0081","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-014-4-1-core-patterns","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s013","q":"How would you combine few-shot examples with chain of thought?","a":"<p>Write each worked example so that it includes the reasoning steps and then the final answer, and end the new prompt where the reasoning should start (for instance \"Let's think step by step\"). The model then copies the format: reasoning, then answer. Keep the final-answer format fixed so it can be parsed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0082","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-014-4-1-core-patterns","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s013","q":"A user sends a vague request. Which prompt pattern would you reach for?","a":"<p>Use the cognitive verifier to ask clarifying questions, or question refinement to propose a sharper question. If the vague request still needs an answer, a role plus explicit output-format constraints narrows the response. Few-shot is for format problems, not for missing information.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0083","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-015-4-2-templates-and-context-management","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s014","q":"Why use prompt templates instead of building prompt strings inline?","a":"<p>Templates separate the fixed instruction from the changing inputs. They give consistent wording, can be versioned and reviewed like code, are testable, and let non-developers edit prompts. Libraries such as LangChain's <code>PromptTemplate</code> or Jinja add validation and composition.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>templates = {\n    &quot;translation&quot;: &quot;Translate from {source} to {target}: {text}&quot;,\n    &quot;summary&quot;: &quot;Summarize the following in {length} words: {text}&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0084","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-015-4-2-templates-and-context-management","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s014","q":"What are the pitfalls of using Python <code>str.format</code> for prompt templates?","a":"<p>A missing variable raises <code>KeyError</code>. Literal curly braces, such as a JSON schema in the prompt, must be doubled (<code>{{</code> and <code>}}</code>). And user text inserted into the prompt can contain instructions, which is a prompt-injection risk, so separate it clearly (delimiters, role messages) and treat it as untrusted.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>template = &quot;Translate from {source} to {target}: {text}&quot;\nprompt = template.format(source=&quot;English&quot;, target=&quot;Spanish&quot;, text=&quot;Hello&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0085","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-015-4-2-templates-and-context-management","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s014","q":"What is wrong with the context-trimming logic in this code?","a":"<p>It counts words, not tokens, so the budget is wrong, and a word is often more than one token. The number of messages kept comes from an arbitrary <code>max_tokens//100</code>, ignoring how long each message is. Count with the model's tokenizer, then drop the oldest messages until the total fits, and leave room for the reply.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def manage_context(messages, max_tokens=4000):\n    total_tokens = sum(len(msg.split()) for msg in messages)\n\n    if total_tokens &gt; max_tokens:\n        # Keep system message and recent messages\n        return [messages[0]] + messages[-(max_tokens//100):]\n    return messages</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0086","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-015-4-2-templates-and-context-management","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s014","q":"Why always keep the system message when trimming conversation history?","a":"<p>The system message holds the rules, role and output format. If it is dropped, the assistant forgets its instructions and drifts. The oldest turns are the cheapest to lose, because the latest ones matter most for the current answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0087","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-015-4-2-templates-and-context-management","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s014","q":"What can you do when a conversation exceeds the model's context window?","a":"<p>Truncate or use a sliding window of the last N turns, summarise older turns into a short running summary, retrieve only the relevant past messages or documents (retrieval-augmented generation), or move to a long-context model. Each trades completeness against cost and latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0088","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-015-4-2-templates-and-context-management","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s014","q":"Why does the position of information in a long prompt matter?","a":"<p>Studies (\"Lost in the Middle\", Liu et al., 2023) found that models use information at the start and end of a long context better than information in the middle. Put the instructions and the key facts near the start or end, and keep the context short and relevant rather than filling the window.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0089","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-016-5-few-shot-in-context-learning","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s015","q":"What is few-shot (in-context) learning, and does the model's weights change?","a":"<p>You include a few worked input-output examples in the prompt, and the model infers the task and format from them and continues the pattern. No weights are updated: the examples only condition that one request, and the model forgets them afterwards. In-context learning and few-shot prompting mean essentially the same thing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0090","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-016-5-few-shot-in-context-learning","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s015","q":"What is the difference between zero-shot, one-shot and few-shot prompting?","a":"<p>Zero-shot gives only the instruction. One-shot gives a single example, and few-shot gives several. More examples usually fix the output format and style better, at the price of a longer prompt (more cost and latency) and less room in the context window.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0091","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-016-5-few-shot-in-context-learning","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s015","q":"When would you choose few-shot prompting over fine-tuning, and when the reverse?","a":"<p>Few-shot prompting needs no training, so it is quick to iterate and works with a handful of examples. Fine-tuning suits tasks with hundreds or thousands of labelled examples, a stable task, and a need for lower per-request cost, since examples no longer travel in every prompt. Prompting wins for fast changes, fine-tuning for scale and consistency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0092","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-016-5-few-shot-in-context-learning","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s015","q":"How many examples should a few-shot prompt contain, and what matters more than the count?","a":"<p>Usually 2 to 5. Quality and consistency matter more: examples should be correct, representative of real inputs, cover the main cases or labels, and use exactly the format you want back. Gains flatten after a handful of examples, while the prompt keeps growing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0093","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-016-5-few-shot-in-context-learning","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s015","q":"What biases affect few-shot prompts?","a":"<p>Models can favour the label that appears most often in the examples, the label of the last example (recency), or common tokens. Balance labels, shuffle the order, and test with different orderings. Results can change noticeably when only the order of the examples changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0094","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-016-5-few-shot-in-context-learning","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s015","q":"What are the drawbacks of few-shot prompting?","a":"<p>The examples are re-sent on every request, so cost and latency go up. They use up context window, and the benefit depends on example choice and order. Results are less stable than a fine-tuned model, and there is no guarantee on format without validation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0095","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-017-5-1-notebook-few-shot-summarisation-tran","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s016","q":"In few-shot prompting, why are the examples and the new query joined with <code>\"\\n\\n\"</code> and kept in one consistent format?","a":"<p>Blank lines separate the examples and show the model where one ends and the next begins. An identical format in every example makes the pattern obvious, so the model copies it for the new query. Mixed formats confuse it and make the output format drift.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>combined_prompt = &quot;\\n\\n&quot;.join(few_shot_examples + [prompt])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0096","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-017-5-1-notebook-few-shot-summarisation-tran","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s016","q":"For question answering, why should a few-shot example include the context, the question and the answer?","a":"<p>A good example shows the full task shape: read the context, find the answer in it, and write it in the target style, such as a full sentence rather than a bare span. The model copies both the structure and the answer style, which also reduces answers invented from outside the context.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>few_shot_examples = [\n    &quot;The capital of France is Paris. question: &#x27;What is the capital of France?&#x27; answer: &#x27;The capital of France is Paris.&#x27;&quot;\n]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0097","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-018-5-2-generic-few-shot-tooling","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s017","q":"How would you choose the most relevant examples for each new input automatically?","a":"<p>Embed the new input and all candidate examples with a sentence-embedding model, then take the nearest neighbours by cosine similarity (an index such as FAISS speeds this up). Showing the model examples close to the query usually works better than fixed examples. Add diversity (for instance maximal marginal relevance) so the chosen examples do not all repeat the same case.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def select_best_examples(task_type, new_input, example_pool, num_examples=2):\n    selected = example_pool[:num_examples]  # In practice, use similarity metrics\n    return create_few_shot_prompt(task_type, selected, new_input)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0098","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-018-5-2-generic-few-shot-tooling","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s017","q":"In LangChain, what does <code>SemanticSimilarityExampleSelector</code> do?","a":"<p>It stores your example pool as embeddings in a vector store and, at run time, returns the k examples most similar to the incoming input, which the prompt template then inserts as few-shot examples. It automates dynamic example selection and avoids hand-picking examples.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0099","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-018-5-2-generic-few-shot-tooling","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s017","q":"Why is taking the first N items from the pool a poor selection strategy?","a":"<p>It ignores the query: the examples may be about a different topic or label, cover only some cases, and always be the same. Selection by similarity, by label coverage, or by testing candidates on a validation set gives better results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0100","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-018-5-2-generic-few-shot-tooling","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s017","q":"How should the examples be delimited and formatted inside a prompt?","a":"<p>Use the same fixed template for every example: a label for the input, a label for the output, and a blank line or a marker between examples. The new input then uses the same label and leaves the output empty. Consistent markers help the model and make parsing the answer easier.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for ex in examples:\n    example_str += f&quot;{task_type}: {ex[&#x27;input&#x27;]}\\n{ex[&#x27;output&#x27;]}\\n\\n&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0101","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-019-5-2-generic-few-shot-tooling","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s017","q":"What makes a good few-shot example, and how would you clean a pool of examples?","a":"<p>A good example has a correct output, is typical of real inputs, and follows the target format exactly. Clean the pool by removing duplicates, empty or wrong outputs and inputs that are too short, checking labels, and balancing classes. Simple rules like length checks catch obvious errors only, so review a sample by hand.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>if len(ex[&#x27;input&#x27;]) &gt; 10 and len(ex[&#x27;output&#x27;]) &gt; 0:\n    if task_type == &quot;summarize&quot; and len(ex[&#x27;output&#x27;]) &lt; len(ex[&#x27;input&#x27;]):\n        valid_examples.append(ex)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0102","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-019-5-2-generic-few-shot-tooling","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s017","q":"How would you keep a few-shot prompt within a token budget?","a":"<p>Count tokens with the model's tokenizer instead of the rough rule of about 4 characters per token for English (other languages need more). When over budget, drop or shorten examples, shorten instructions, or summarise the context, keeping the actual input intact. Leave room for the output tokens too.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>estimated_tokens = len(prompt) // 4</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0103","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-019-5-2-generic-few-shot-tooling","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s017","q":"Is output length a valid measure of whether few-shot prompting improved a result?","a":"<p>No. A longer answer is not a better one. Compare zero-shot and few-shot on a labelled test set with a task metric (accuracy, ROUGE or BLEU for text, or human or model-based ratings), over enough inputs to see a real difference, and watch the extra token cost.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>...\n&quot;improvement&quot;: len(few_shot_result) &gt; len(zero_shot_result)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0104","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-020-6-prompt-engineering-case-studies","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s018","q":"Why do specific, structured prompts beat generic ones, and what do you add to make a prompt specific?","a":"<p>A generic prompt leaves the model guessing the goal, audience and format, so answers wander. Add context (who and what for), explicit steps or fields to cover, constraints and the output format. In customer service, for instance, replacing a generic greeting with a short menu of options routes users faster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0105","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-020-6-prompt-engineering-case-studies","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s018","q":"In a support bot, why present category options in the first message?","a":"<p>A menu reduces ambiguity: the user's choice acts as an intent, so the bot can route to the right flow or agent without guessing. It saves turns and avoids wrong answers. Keep an \"other\" option and escalation to a human for cases the menu does not cover.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>return &quot;&quot;&quot;Welcome to {company_name} support. For faster assistance, please select from the following options:\n1. Account issue\n2. Technical support  \n3. Billing inquiry\n...</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0106","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-020-6-prompt-engineering-case-studies","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s018","q":"How would you design an LLM prompt for a symptom-checker style health assistant, and what safeguards matter?","a":"<p>Collect structured inputs (symptoms, duration, severity, history, tests), ask for reasoned possibilities and next steps instead of a diagnosis, and tell the user to see a clinician. Safeguards: human review, disclaimers, red-flag escalation (for instance chest pain), grounding in vetted clinical references, logging, and privacy controls. Patient data is protected health information, so do not send it to a provider without the proper agreement (such as a HIPAA business associate agreement).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>prompt = f&quot;&quot;&quot;Medical Analysis Request:\nSymptoms: {symptoms}\nRecent Tests: {tests}\nMedical History: {history}&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0107","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-020-6-prompt-engineering-case-studies","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s018","q":"Which risks make healthcare prompts more sensitive than a casual chatbot?","a":"<p>Wrong or invented advice can harm people, patient data is regulated, and users may trust confident wording. Reduce risk with grounding in retrieved clinical references, structured output, uncertainty statements, human-in-the-loop review, evaluation on expert-labelled cases, and audit logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0108","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-021-6-prompt-engineering-case-studies","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s018","q":"How would you prompt an LLM to summarise news without missing controversies?","a":"<p>List what the summary must contain (facts, opposing views, implications), set a length, and put the article in a clearly delimited block. A generic \"summarise this\" prompt tends to flatten the story to one side. Ask the model to stay faithful to the text and not add outside facts, and spot-check summaries against the article for dropped or invented claims.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>return f&quot;&quot;&quot;Summarize the key points and any controversies from the following article, ensuring to cover all critical information concisely:\n\nArticle: {article_text}\n\nFocus on:\n- Main facts and developments\n- Any controversies or opposing viewpoints\n- Implications and significance\n- Critical details that impact understanding&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0109","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-021-6-prompt-engineering-case-studies","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s018","q":"What are hallucination risks in summarisation, and how do you reduce them?","a":"<p>A summary can add facts that are not in the text, or distort them. Reduce this by instructing the model to use only the provided text, asking for quotes or evidence, using low temperature, and checking faithfulness automatically (entailment models, QA-based checks) and with human review.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0110","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-021-6-prompt-engineering-case-studies","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s018","q":"Why add a scenario or register to a translation prompt?","a":"<p>Translation depends on tone, formality and setting, such as the polite \"usted\" or casual \"tú\" in Spanish and French. Giving the scenario and audience steers the model toward natural, appropriate wording instead of a literal rendering.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>return f&quot;&quot;&quot;Translate the following sentence as if you were {context}:\n\nSentence: &quot;{sentence}&quot;\nTarget Language: {target_language}\nContext: {context}\n&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0111","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-022-6-prompt-engineering-case-studies","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s018","q":"How should an organisation manage many prompts across domains?","a":"<p>Keep prompts in a central library organised by domain and purpose, with named placeholders, version control, review, tests and an owner. Record which version is in production and what metrics it achieved, so changes can be rolled back.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>templates = {\n    &#x27;customer_service&#x27;: {\n        &#x27;greeting&#x27;: &quot;Welcome to {company}. How can I assist you with: {options}?&quot;,\n        &#x27;routing&#x27;: &quot;For {issue_type}, please provide: {required_info}&quot;\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0112","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-022-6-prompt-engineering-case-studies","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s018","q":"What makes a prompt \"optimised\" compared with a generic one?","a":"<p>It is specific about the task, supplies context, defines the expected output (format, length, fields), and sets the audience or tone. For instance, <code>Translate '{input}'</code> is vague, while adding the situation and tone gives the model enough to choose wording.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0113","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-023-6-prompt-engineering-case-studies","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s018","q":"Are keyword checks a reliable way to score prompt quality?","a":"<p>No. They count surface features: a long prompt with 'please' scores well and says nothing about whether it works. Judge prompts by outputs: run them on a test set and measure with metrics, rubrics or human review. Heuristics are fine only as a quick lint.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>quality_checks = {\n    &#x27;specificity&#x27;: len(prompt.split()) &gt; 10,\n    &#x27;clarity&#x27;: &#x27;?&#x27; in prompt or &#x27;please&#x27; in prompt.lower(),\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0114","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-023-6-prompt-engineering-case-studies","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s018","q":"A function compares two prompts and declares the one with the higher average score the winner. What is missing?","a":"<p>Any measure of significance. A small difference on a few cases can be noise, especially because LLM output varies. Use enough varied test cases, the same inputs for both prompts, repeated runs, confidence intervals or a paired significance test, and look at per-case regressions and cost, not only the mean.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>winner = &#x27;prompt_a&#x27; if results[&#x27;prompt_a&#x27;][&#x27;avg&#x27;] &gt; results[&#x27;prompt_b&#x27;][&#x27;avg&#x27;] else &#x27;prompt_b&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0115","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-023-6-prompt-engineering-case-studies","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s018","q":"How would you A/B test prompts for an LLM feature?","a":"<p>Define the metric first. Run both prompts on a fixed offline test set and compare. Then route a share of real traffic to each variant, with random assignment, track the metric and guardrail metrics (cost, latency, error rate), and decide with enough sample size. Keep the winner and keep a regression set for later changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0116","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-023-6-prompt-engineering-case-studies","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s018","q":"What does a safe loop for improving prompts in production look like?","a":"<p>Analyse current failures, find user pain points, design a more specific prompt, test it offline against the current one, run a staged online test, measure impact on the key metrics, then repeat. Version every prompt and keep a test set that grows with each failure found.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0117","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-024-7-challenge-few-shot-article-translation","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s019","q":"Why do long documents plus few-shot examples cause trouble, and how do you handle it?","a":"<p>Examples, the document and the output all share the model's context window (T5 models were trained with 512-token inputs), so a long article leaves no room. Options: split the document into paragraphs or sentences and translate each, use short examples, summarise first when a summary is acceptable, or choose a longer-context model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0118","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-024-7-challenge-few-shot-article-translation","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s019","q":"When would you use a dedicated machine-translation model instead of few-shot prompting an LLM?","a":"<p>When quality, cost and speed on one language pair matter at volume. Dedicated models (MarianMT, NLLB, commercial services) are smaller, faster and usually stronger for common pairs. Few-shot prompting fits when you need a particular style or glossary, have no MT model for the pair, or want to try an idea quickly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0119","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-024-7-challenge-few-shot-article-translation","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s019","q":"In Hugging Face <code>datasets</code>, what does the second argument of <code>load_dataset</code> select?","a":"<p>The first argument is the dataset on the Hub. The second is the configuration (subset or version) of that dataset. The call downloads and caches the data and returns a <code>DatasetDict</code> with splits such as <code>train</code>, <code>validation</code> and <code>test</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from datasets import load_dataset\n\ndataset = load_dataset(&#x27;abisee/cnn_dailymail&#x27;, &#x27;3.0.0&#x27;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0120","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-025-7-challenge-few-shot-article-translation","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s019","q":"How should you structure a few-shot translation prompt so the model starts translating the new text?","a":"<p>Repeat the same block for every example: the instruction, labelled source text, labelled translation, a clear separator. For the new text, use the same block but stop right after the <code>Translation:</code> label, so the next tokens the model writes are the translation. Same labels and layout in every block is what makes the pattern easy to copy.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>few_shot_prompt = &quot;&quot;\n\nfor example in articles[:2]:\n    few_shot_prompt += f&quot;Translate this text from English to Spanish:\\n\\n Text: \\n\\n {example[&#x27;article&#x27;]} \\n\\n Translation: \\n\\n {example[&#x27;translation&#x27;]} \\n\\n &quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0121","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-026-7-challenge-few-shot-article-translation","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s019","q":"What is risky about building few-shot translation examples with the same model that will do the translating?","a":"<p>Any error the model makes is placed in the examples as if it were correct, and then the model copies it, so mistakes repeat and are reinforced. Use human or independently verified translations for the examples.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def translate_text(text):\n    prompt = f&quot;translate English to Spanish: {text}&quot;\n    inputs = tokenizer(prompt, return_tensors=&quot;tf&quot;, max_length=512, \n                      truncation=True, padding=True)\n    outputs = model.generate(inputs[&quot;input_ids&quot;], max_length=200, \n                           num_beams=5, early_stopping=True)\n    return tokenizer.decode(outputs[0], skip_special_tokens=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0122","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-026-7-challenge-few-shot-article-translation","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s019","q":"How can context such as tone be preserved in few-shot translation?","a":"<p>Include examples whose style matches the target, state the desired tone and audience in the instruction, and attach a short note about what to preserve (formal, casual, technical). The model then copies the register in its translation.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>enhanced_examples = [\n    {\n        &#x27;english&#x27;: &quot;Hey, what&#x27;s up?&quot;,\n        &#x27;spanish&#x27;: &quot;Oye, ¿qué tal?&quot;,\n        &#x27;context_note&#x27;: &quot;Casual tone preserved&quot;\n    }\n]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0123","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-026-7-challenge-few-shot-article-translation","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s019","q":"In what order should a few-shot prompt be assembled?","a":"<p>Instruction first, then the examples, then the new input with the output label left empty. Putting the new input last keeps it closest to where the model starts writing and avoids it being cut by truncation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0124","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-027-7-challenge-few-shot-article-translation","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s019","q":"What are the weaknesses of heuristics such as length ratio and \"differs from the source\" for validating translations, and what is better?","a":"<p>They catch empty output, copied source text and runaway length, but a fluent translation with the wrong meaning passes. Add a language-identification check on the output, reference-based metrics such as BLEU, chrF or COMET on a test set, back-translation comparison, glossary checks for names and numbers, and human review for a sample.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>validation_results = {\n    &#x27;non_empty&#x27;: len(translation.strip()) &gt; 0,\n    &#x27;different_from_original&#x27;: translation.lower() != original.lower(),\n    &#x27;appropriate_length&#x27;: 0.3 &lt;= len(translation)/len(original) &lt;= 2.0,\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0125","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-027-7-challenge-few-shot-article-translation","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s019","q":"When a model has limited GPU memory, how can you still run few-shot translation?","a":"<p>Use a smaller checkpoint, shorter inputs, fewer beams and fewer or shorter examples, or load the model in half precision or quantised. These lower memory and time but usually lower quality, so check that the output still meets the bar.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>small_tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(&quot;google/flan-t5-small&quot;)\nsmall_model = TFAutoModelForSeq2SeqLM.from_pretrained(&quot;google/flan-t5-small&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0126","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-027-7-challenge-few-shot-article-translation","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s019","q":"What are the steps and risks of a pipeline that summarises first and then translates?","a":"<p>Step 1 produces a short summary of the article, and step 2 translates it with few-shot examples. Risks: errors compound across the two calls, details are lost, and each call adds latency and cost. Validate after each step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0127","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-028-8-transfer-learning-vs-fine-tuning","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s020","q":"What is the difference between transfer learning and fine-tuning, as the terms are often used?","a":"<p>In the narrow sense, transfer learning keeps a pre-trained model's base frozen and trains only a new task-specific head, while fine-tuning updates all (or many) weights on the new data. Usage varies: many people call any reuse of a pre-trained model transfer learning, and fine-tuning is then one form of it. In an interview, state which meaning you use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0128","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-028-8-transfer-learning-vs-fine-tuning","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s020","q":"Why does transfer learning work with small datasets?","a":"<p>A pre-trained model has already learned general features from a large dataset (edges and textures in images, grammar and meaning in text). Those transfer to a related task, so only a small part needs learning from your data. This needs less data, time and compute than training from scratch and usually generalises better.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0129","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-028-8-transfer-learning-vs-fine-tuning","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s020","q":"When do you freeze the base model, and when do you fine-tune all weights?","a":"<p>Freeze when the new task is close to the pre-training task, data is small, or compute is limited, so the base keeps its knowledge and overfits less. Fine-tune all weights when the domain differs, you have lots of labelled data, and accuracy matters most. The price is more compute, more overfitting risk and a need for a small learning rate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0130","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-028-8-transfer-learning-vs-fine-tuning","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s020","q":"Why use a lower learning rate when fine-tuning a pre-trained model?","a":"<p>Large updates overwrite the useful pre-trained weights (catastrophic forgetting) and make training unstable. Small rates, typically 1e-5 to 5e-5 for transformers, adjust them gently. A newly initialised head can use a larger rate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0131","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-028-8-transfer-learning-vs-fine-tuning","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s020","q":"You have 500 labelled images in a niche domain. How do you approach it?","a":"<p>Start from a model pre-trained on a large dataset such as ImageNet, freeze the base and train a new head, with data augmentation. Compare with a simple baseline. If accuracy is limited, unfreeze the top layers and continue at a low learning rate. Use a held-out test set and avoid training the full network from scratch, which would overfit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0132","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-028-8-transfer-learning-vs-fine-tuning","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s020","q":"What factors decide the transfer strategy?","a":"<p>Task similarity to the pre-training domain, amount of labelled data, and compute budget. High similarity with little data favours a frozen base. Medium similarity with enough compute favours progressive unfreezing. Low similarity with lots of data favours full fine-tuning. These are rules of thumb, so confirm with experiments.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>if task_similarity == &#x27;high&#x27; and data_size == &#x27;small&#x27;:\n    strategy = &#x27;transfer_learning_frozen&#x27;\nelif task_similarity == &#x27;medium&#x27; and computational_budget == &#x27;high&#x27;:\n    strategy = &#x27;progressive_fine_tuning&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0133","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-028-8-transfer-learning-vs-fine-tuning","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s020","q":"How do prompting, a frozen-base transfer approach and full fine-tuning compare for adapting an LLM?","a":"<p>Prompting changes no weights: cheapest and fastest, but depends on prompt quality and needs long prompts. Training a head or a few layers needs labelled data and moderate compute. Full fine-tuning gives the strongest task fit but needs the most data, compute and storage for a full model copy per task. Start with prompting, then move up only if quality or cost requires it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0134","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-029-8-1-generic-freeze-the-base-pattern-kera","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s021","q":"In Keras, what does <code>base_model.trainable = not freeze_base</code> do in this transfer-learning helper?","a":"<p>Setting <code>trainable = False</code> stops the weights of the base model from being updated during training, so only the new layers (the pooling and dense head) learn. With <code>freeze_base=False</code> the whole base is trained as well.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def create_transfer_model(base_model, num_classes, freeze_base=True):\n    base_model.trainable = not freeze_base\n\n    model = tf.keras.Sequential([\n        base_model,\n        tf.keras.layers.GlobalAveragePooling2D(),\n        tf.keras.layers.Dense(num_classes, activation=&#x27;softmax&#x27;)\n    ])\n\n    return model</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0135","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-029-8-1-generic-freeze-the-base-pattern-kera","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s021","q":"Why use <code>GlobalAveragePooling2D</code> rather than <code>Flatten</code> between a convolutional base and the classifier?","a":"<p>It averages each feature map to one number, producing a vector of the channel count. That gives far fewer parameters in the next dense layer than flattening the whole spatial grid, which reduces overfitting on small datasets, and the model no longer depends on the exact input size.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0136","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-029-8-1-generic-freeze-the-base-pattern-kera","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s021","q":"Why does the classifier head use softmax with <code>num_classes</code> outputs?","a":"<p>Softmax turns the outputs into probabilities across mutually exclusive classes, summing to 1, and pairs with categorical cross-entropy. For binary tasks use one sigmoid unit with binary cross-entropy. For multi-label problems use sigmoid per label.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0137","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-029-8-1-generic-freeze-the-base-pattern-kera","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s021","q":"What does <code>include_top=False</code> do when you load a Keras application model?","a":"<p>It removes the original classifier layers (for instance the 1000-class ImageNet head) and returns the convolutional feature extractor, so you can attach your own head with the right number of outputs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0138","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-030-8-2-lecture-examples-across-domains","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s022","q":"Why can an ImageNet-trained model help detect pneumonia in chest X-rays?","a":"<p>Early layers learn generic visual features such as edges, textures and shapes that are useful in any image domain, so they transfer even though the subject differs. Medical datasets are small, so reusing these features beats training from scratch. The gap between photographs and X-rays is a reason to fine-tune the higher layers later.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>base_model = VGG19(weights=&#x27;imagenet&#x27;, include_top=False, input_shape=(224, 224, 3))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0139","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-030-8-2-lecture-examples-across-domains","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s022","q":"Why does the input shape use 3 channels, and what do you do with grayscale X-rays?","a":"<p>ImageNet models expect RGB input with 3 channels, so their first layer has weights for three. Grayscale images are repeated across three channels (or the first layer is adapted). Also apply the same preprocessing as the pre-trained model (for instance <code>preprocess_input</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0140","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-030-8-2-lecture-examples-across-domains","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s022","q":"What is the difference between a head with <code>Flatten</code> plus a large <code>Dense</code> layer and a head with global pooling and dropout?","a":"<p>Flattening VGG's 7x7x512 output gives 25,088 values, so a Dense(512) after it has about 12.8 million weights, which overfits easily. Global average pooling gives 512 values. Dropout randomly turns off units during training to reduce co-adaptation. The pooled, regularised head is a better default for small datasets.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tf.keras.layers.GlobalAveragePooling2D(),\ntf.keras.layers.Dropout(0.5),\ntf.keras.layers.Dense(512, activation=&#x27;relu&#x27;),</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0141","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-030-8-2-lecture-examples-across-domains","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s022","q":"For a binary medical classifier, how is the output layer set up and which loss is used?","a":"<p>One sigmoid unit outputs the probability of the positive class, paired with <code>binary_crossentropy</code>. A threshold (default 0.5) turns the probability into a label, and the threshold can be tuned.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tf.keras.layers.Dense(1, activation=&#x27;sigmoid&#x27;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0142","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-030-8-2-lecture-examples-across-domains","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s022","q":"Why is accuracy not enough for a pneumonia detector?","a":"<p>Medical data is usually imbalanced, so a model that predicts \"healthy\" for everyone can look accurate. Missing a sick patient costs far more than a false alarm. Report recall (sensitivity), precision, specificity and AUC, look at the confusion matrix, and tune the decision threshold for the clinical cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0143","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-031-8-2-lecture-examples-across-domains","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s022","q":"Why is a pre-trained BERT a good start for sentiment analysis with limited review data?","a":"<p>BERT's pre-training on large text corpora gave it knowledge of language, so a small labelled set only has to teach it the sentiment mapping. You add a classification head and fine-tune, which usually beats a model trained on the small dataset alone.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model = TFBertForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained(&#x27;bert-base-uncased&#x27;, num_labels=2)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0144","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-031-8-2-lecture-examples-across-domains","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s022","q":"What label format do <code>sparse_categorical_crossentropy</code> and <code>categorical_crossentropy</code> expect?","a":"<p>Sparse categorical cross-entropy takes integer class labels (0, 1, 2, ...). Categorical cross-entropy takes one-hot vectors. Both compute the same quantity, so choose by how your labels are stored. Models that output logits also need <code>from_logits=True</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model.compile(optimizer=&#x27;adam&#x27;, loss=&#x27;sparse_categorical_crossentropy&#x27;, metrics=[&#x27;accuracy&#x27;])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0145","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-031-8-2-lecture-examples-across-domains","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s022","q":"What is MobileNet designed for, and how does it trade accuracy against size?","a":"<p>MobileNet is built for mobile and embedded devices. It uses depthwise-separable convolutions, which cut parameters and compute a lot compared with standard convolutions (about 4 million parameters against 138 million for VGG16), with a small accuracy loss. It suits on-device inference where latency and memory are tight.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>base_model = MobileNet(weights=&#x27;imagenet&#x27;, include_top=False, input_shape=(224, 224, 3))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0146","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-031-8-2-lecture-examples-across-domains","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s022","q":"How do VGG19, ResNet50 and MobileNet differ when you pick a base model for transfer learning?","a":"<p>VGG19 is simple but very large and slow. ResNet50 uses residual connections, trains deeper networks well and is a strong general-purpose choice. MobileNet is small and fast for edge devices at somewhat lower accuracy. Choose by accuracy needs, latency, memory and deployment target.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0147","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-031-8-2-lecture-examples-across-domains","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s022","q":"When would you pick DistilBERT or multilingual BERT over BERT-base?","a":"<p>DistilBERT when latency or memory matter and a small drop in accuracy is acceptable. Multilingual BERT (mBERT) when the text is in several languages and you want one model. Use a monolingual model when you only need one language and the best quality for it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0148","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-031-8-2-lecture-examples-across-domains","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s022","q":"Why does a transfer-learning image project often use data augmentation?","a":"<p>Small datasets overfit, and augmentation (flips, crops, rotations, colour jitter) creates varied training views of the same labels. It acts as regularisation and helps the model generalise. Choose transformations that keep the label valid: flipping a digit or a medical image may not.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0149","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-032-8-3-progressive-fine-tuning-frozen-first","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s023","q":"Why train only the new head first and unfreeze the base model afterwards?","a":"<p>A randomly initialised head gives large, noisy gradients at first. If the base is trainable, these gradients damage the pre-trained weights. Training the head first gives it sensible values, so when the base is unfrozen later the updates are small and useful.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def progressive_fine_tuning(model, train_data, val_data):\n    # Phase 1: Train only new layers\n    model.compile(optimizer=&#x27;adam&#x27;, loss=&#x27;categorical_crossentropy&#x27;, metrics=[&#x27;accuracy&#x27;])\n    model.fit(train_data, validation_data=val_data, epochs=5)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0150","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-032-8-3-progressive-fine-tuning-frozen-first","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s023","q":"Why recompile after setting <code>trainable = True</code>, and why use a lower learning rate in the second phase?","a":"<p>In Keras the list of trainable weights is fixed when the model is compiled, so changing <code>trainable</code> has no effect on training until you call <code>compile</code> again. The lower learning rate (1e-5) gives small steps, so the already good pre-trained weights are adjusted gently and not overwritten.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model.layers[0].trainable = True  # Unfreeze base model\nmodel.compile(\n    optimizer=tf.keras.optimizers.Adam(1e-5),  # Lower learning rate\n    loss=&#x27;categorical_crossentropy&#x27;,\n    metrics=[&#x27;accuracy&#x27;]\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0151","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-032-8-3-progressive-fine-tuning-frozen-first","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s023","q":"Which layers should you unfreeze first, and how many?","a":"<p>The top layers, nearest the head. Lower layers hold generic features that rarely need changing, while higher layers are more task-specific. Unfreeze gradually (a few layers at a time, as in gradual unfreezing) or use lower learning rates for lower layers (discriminative learning rates), and watch validation loss.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0152","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-032-8-3-progressive-fine-tuning-frozen-first","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s023","q":"What is the pitfall with batch normalisation layers when unfreezing a Keras model for fine-tuning?","a":"<p>Batch-norm layers keep running statistics. When the base model is unfrozen, these statistics can update on a small new batch and degrade the pre-trained behaviour. The Keras guide recommends calling the base model with <code>training=False</code> so batch-norm stays in inference mode, and unfreezing only after the head has been trained.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0153","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-033-8-4-deployment-and-evaluation-helpers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s024","q":"How does TensorFlow Lite convert a model for deployment, and what does the optimisation flag do?","a":"<p><code>TFLiteConverter</code> turns a Keras model into a compact TensorFlow Lite flatbuffer for mobile and edge devices. <code>Optimize.DEFAULT</code> applies post-training quantisation (dynamic range: weights stored as 8-bit integers), which shrinks the file and often speeds up inference with a small accuracy loss. In recent TF 2.x releases <code>OPTIMIZE_FOR_SIZE</code> and <code>OPTIMIZE_FOR_LATENCY</code> are deprecated in favour of <code>DEFAULT</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>converter = tf.lite.TFLiteConverter.from_keras_model(model)\nconverter.optimizations = [tf.lite.Optimize.DEFAULT]\ntflite_model = converter.convert()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0154","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-033-8-4-deployment-and-evaluation-helpers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s024","q":"What kinds of post-training quantisation exist, and what are the trade-offs?","a":"<p>Dynamic range quantisation stores weights as int8 and needs no data. Full integer quantisation also quantises activations using a small calibration dataset, enabling integer-only accelerators. Float16 quantisation halves the size with little loss and works well on GPUs. Quantisation-aware training gives the best accuracy but needs retraining. Lower precision gives smaller and faster models at some accuracy cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0155","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-033-8-4-deployment-and-evaluation-helpers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s024","q":"Besides quantisation, how can you make a model smaller or faster for deployment?","a":"<p>Distil a large model into a smaller student, prune weights that matter little, choose an efficient architecture (MobileNet), reduce input resolution or sequence length, and export to an optimised runtime (TFLite, ONNX Runtime, TensorRT).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0156","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-033-8-4-deployment-and-evaluation-helpers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s024","q":"How should you measure whether transfer learning helped?","a":"<p>Evaluate on a held-out test set the model never trained or tuned on, and compare with a baseline on the same data. A baseline is a simple reference such as a majority-class predictor, a small model or the zero-shot pre-trained model. Without it a score such as 90 percent accuracy has no meaning, because a trivial model may reach it. Report the absolute and relative gain. Use validation data for choices such as epochs and learning rate, and keep the test set for the final number.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>test_loss, test_accuracy = model.evaluate(test_data, verbose=0)\nimprovement = test_accuracy - baseline_accuracy</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0157","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-034-9-transfer-learning-on-flan-t5-en-es-tra","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s025","q":"When can fine-tuning a model still be worthwhile for a task that it already handles zero-shot, such as English to Spanish translation?","a":"<p>When you need better quality in a specific domain, consistent terminology and style, shorter prompts, lower latency or cost with a smaller model, or behaviour that prompting cannot give reliably. Check first whether prompting or few-shot is good enough, because fine-tuning adds data work and maintenance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0158","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-034-9-transfer-learning-on-flan-t5-en-es-tra","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s025","q":"What are the main steps to fine-tune a Hugging Face seq2seq model for translation?","a":"<p>Choose a pre-trained checkpoint and load the tokenizer and model. Load a parallel dataset. Preprocess: add the task prefix, tokenize inputs and targets, mask padding in the labels. Decide which layers to train. Compile or configure the trainer with a small learning rate. Train with validation. Evaluate with BLEU or ROUGE and spot-check outputs. Save and deploy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0159","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-034-9-transfer-learning-on-flan-t5-en-es-tra","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s025","q":"Why start with <code>flan-t5-base</code> rather than the largest model when you fine-tune?","a":"<p>Training cost and memory grow with model size, and gradients and optimiser state multiply the memory need. A base-size model fits a single GPU, iterates faster and often reaches good quality on a narrow task. Move to a larger model only when the smaller one is not good enough.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0160","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-035-9-1-load-preprocess-convert","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s026","q":"What does each step of this seq2seq preprocessing do?","a":"<p>It adds a task prefix to each source sentence, so the model knows the task, and takes the target sentences. It tokenizes inputs as <code>input_ids</code> and <code>attention_mask</code>, tokenizes targets as <code>labels</code> (what the decoder must produce), and returns them so <code>map</code> can add the columns to the dataset.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def preprocess_data(examples):\n    inputs = [f&#x27;Translate from English to Spanish: {example[&quot;en&quot;]}&#x27; for example in examples[&#x27;translation&#x27;]]\n    targets = [example[&#x27;es&#x27;] for example in examples[&#x27;translation&#x27;]]\n    ...\n    model_inputs[&quot;labels&quot;] = labels[&quot;input_ids&quot;]\n    return model_inputs</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0161","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-035-9-1-load-preprocess-convert","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s026","q":"In seq2seq fine-tuning, is it right to pass the unchanged target ids as <code>decoder_input_ids</code>?","a":"<p>No. With teacher forcing the decoder input must be the labels shifted right by one position, starting with the decoder start token (the pad id for T5). Hugging Face builds this automatically when you pass only <code>labels</code>. Giving the unshifted targets lets the decoder see at each position the token it must predict, so loss looks artificially low and generation is poor.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model_inputs[&quot;labels&quot;] = labels[&quot;input_ids&quot;]\n\n# For decoder inputs\ndecoder_inputs = tokenizer(targets, max_length=128, truncation=True, padding=&quot;max_length&quot;)\nmodel_inputs[&quot;decoder_input_ids&quot;] = decoder_inputs[&quot;input_ids&quot;]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0162","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-035-9-1-load-preprocess-convert","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s026","q":"Why mask padding positions in the labels with -100?","a":"<p>Loss functions in Hugging Face ignore label positions set to -100. If padding tokens count in the loss, the model is rewarded for predicting pad and the loss is distorted by how much padding a batch has. Replace the pad ids in <code>labels</code> with -100 yourself; <code>DataCollatorForSeq2Seq</code> then pads shorter label sequences in a batch with -100 (its <code>label_pad_token_id</code>), it does not convert existing pad ids.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0163","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-035-9-1-load-preprocess-convert","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s026","q":"What is the difference between <code>padding=\"max_length\"</code> and dynamic padding?","a":"<p><code>padding=\"max_length\"</code> pads every example to the same fixed length, which wastes compute on short examples. Dynamic padding pads each batch only to its longest sequence, using a data collator, and is faster, especially when lengths vary. Fixed length is simpler and gives static shapes, which some accelerators need.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0164","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-035-9-1-load-preprocess-convert","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s026","q":"How do you tokenize the target text for a seq2seq model in current transformers?","a":"<p>Use the <code>text_target</code> argument: <code>tokenizer(inputs, text_target=targets, ...)</code>. It returns <code>labels</code> in the same call. The older <code>with tokenizer.as_target_tokenizer():</code> context manager is deprecated in recent versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0165","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-035-9-1-load-preprocess-convert","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s026","q":"What does <code>Dataset.map(..., batched=True)</code> do, and why use it?","a":"<p><code>batched=True</code> passes batches of rows to the function instead of one row at a time, which is much faster with fast tokenizers. <code>select(range(30000))</code> takes a subset to save time. Results are cached on disk.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>train_dataset = dataset[&#x27;train&#x27;].select(range(30000)).map(preprocess_data, batched=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0166","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-035-9-1-load-preprocess-convert","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s026","q":"What does <code>to_tf_dataset</code> do, and why shuffle only the training data?","a":"<p>It converts a Hugging Face dataset into a <code>tf.data.Dataset</code> that yields batches. <code>columns</code> are the model inputs and <code>label_cols</code> the targets. Shuffling the training data breaks any order in the data and improves training. Evaluation data does not need it, and a fixed order makes comparisons repeatable.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>train_dataset = train_dataset.to_tf_dataset(\n    columns=[&#x27;input_ids&#x27;, &#x27;attention_mask&#x27;, &#x27;decoder_input_ids&#x27;],\n    label_cols=[&#x27;labels&#x27;],\n    shuffle=True,\n    batch_size=64,\n    collate_fn=None\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0167","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-035-9-1-load-preprocess-convert","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s026","q":"How would you choose the tokenizer <code>max_length</code> for a translation dataset?","a":"<p>Look at the token-length distribution of inputs and targets and pick a value covering most examples (for instance the 95th to 99th percentile). A value too small truncates and corrupts targets, and one too large wastes memory and time on padding.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0168","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-036-9-2-freeze-the-pre-trained-layers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s027","q":"What are the advantages and drawbacks of freezing the pre-trained layers during fine-tuning?","a":"<p>Advantages: far fewer trainable parameters, faster training, less memory for gradients and optimiser state, less overfitting, and the pre-trained knowledge is preserved. Drawbacks: the model has less capacity to adapt, so quality may plateau at a level lower than full fine-tuning when the new domain differs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0169","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-036-9-2-freeze-the-pre-trained-layers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s027","q":"How do you freeze parts of a Hugging Face Keras model, and how do you check it worked?","a":"<p>Set <code>trainable = False</code> on the layers, then call <code>model.summary()</code> and compare trainable and non-trainable parameter counts before and after. Set the flags before compiling, or recompile after. If the counts do not change, the freeze did not apply. With the shared embedding, encoder and decoder frozen, only the language-model head (the output projection to the vocabulary) stays trainable in FLAN-T5, so capacity to adapt is limited.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model.get_layer(&quot;shared&quot;).trainable = False\nmodel.get_layer(&quot;encoder&quot;).trainable = False\nmodel.get_layer(&quot;decoder&quot;).trainable = False</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0170","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-036-9-2-freeze-the-pre-trained-layers","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s027","q":"Why must <code>from_logits=True</code> be set for Hugging Face models in Keras?","a":"<p>Hugging Face models output raw logits, with no softmax. With the default <code>from_logits=False</code> Keras assumes probabilities and computes the wrong loss. Setting <code>from_logits=True</code> applies softmax inside the loss, which is also more numerically stable.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>loss=tf.keras.losses.SparseCategoricalCrossentropy(from_logits=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0171","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-037-9-3-train-and-translate","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s028","q":"What is the default Adam learning rate in Keras, and what value does this fine-tuning code use instead?","a":"<p>The Keras default for Adam is 0.001. This code sets 2e-5, which is in the usual 1e-5 to 5e-5 range for fine-tuning pre-trained transformers.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model.compile(optimizer=tf_keras.optimizers.Adam(learning_rate=2e-5),\n              loss=tf.keras.losses.SparseCategoricalCrossentropy(from_logits=True))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0172","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-037-9-3-train-and-translate","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s028","q":"Training loss is not decreasing when fine-tuning. What do you check?","a":"<p>First the learning rate (too high diverges, too low barely moves). Then preprocessing: correct labels, prefix, padding masked in labels, shifted decoder inputs, tokenizer matching the model. Then which layers are trainable, whether the loss is set up properly (<code>from_logits</code>), the batch size and data quality. Overfit a tiny subset to verify the pipeline can learn.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0173","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-037-9-3-train-and-translate","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s028","q":"Translation quality is poor after training. What do you check?","a":"<p>Verify the reference translations are correct and aligned, check tokenisation (truncation of long sentences, padding handling), decode with beam search and a sensible <code>max_length</code>, and confirm train and test inputs use the same prefix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0174","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-037-9-3-train-and-translate","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s028","q":"What is the difference between <code>model.generate</code> and calling the model directly?","a":"<p>Calling <code>model(...)</code> runs one forward pass and returns logits, which is what training uses (with teacher forcing). <code>generate</code> runs the autoregressive loop: it predicts one token at a time, feeds it back, applies the decoding strategy (greedy, beam, sampling) and stops at the end token or length limit.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>outputs = model.generate(inputs[0][&quot;input_ids&quot;], max_length=128, num_beams=4, early_stopping=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0175","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-037-9-3-train-and-translate","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s028","q":"If only 10 percent of parameters are trainable, will training be 10 times faster?","a":"<p>No. The forward pass still runs through the whole network, so that cost stays. You save the backward pass through frozen layers and the memory for their gradients and Adam states, which makes steps cheaper, but the total speedup is much smaller than 10x.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0176","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-038-10-evaluation-metrics-rouge-bleu","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s029","q":"What is the difference between ROUGE and BLEU, and which tasks is each used for?","a":"<p>ROUGE is recall-oriented: how much of the reference text did the model output capture? BLEU is precision-oriented: how much of the model output appears in the reference, using exact n-gram matches (1 to 4 words) and a brevity penalty. ROUGE is the usual choice for summarisation, BLEU for machine translation. They are often reported together because they look at opposite sides of the overlap.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0177","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-038-10-evaluation-metrics-rouge-bleu","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s029","q":"What do ROUGE-1, ROUGE-2 and ROUGE-L measure?","a":"<p>ROUGE-1 counts overlapping unigrams (single words), ROUGE-2 overlapping bigrams (adjacent word pairs, so it rewards some word order), and ROUGE-L uses the longest common subsequence, which rewards words in the same order without needing them to be adjacent. Higher-order n-grams are stricter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0178","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-038-10-evaluation-metrics-rouge-bleu","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s029","q":"Why does BLEU include a brevity penalty?","a":"<p>Precision alone can be gamed: a very short output such as one correct word has 100 percent precision. The brevity penalty lowers the score when the output is shorter than the reference, so the model must also cover the content. BLEU is the n-gram precision (geometric mean of 1- to 4-gram precisions) multiplied by that penalty.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0179","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-038-10-evaluation-metrics-rouge-bleu","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s029","q":"Why can a good translation or summary still get a low BLEU or ROUGE score, and what do you use alongside them?","a":"<p>These metrics only count surface n-gram overlap. A correct paraphrase with different words scores low, and a fluent but wrong sentence that reuses reference words can score high. They also ignore meaning, factual accuracy and fluency. Add embedding-based metrics (BERTScore, COMET for translation), task-specific checks such as factuality for summaries, and human or LLM-as-judge evaluation on a sample.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0180","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-038-10-evaluation-metrics-rouge-bleu","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s029","q":"A summarisation system and a translation system both need an automatic metric. Which would you pick for each and why?","a":"<p>Summarisation: ROUGE, because the question is whether the key content of the reference is covered (recall). Translation: BLEU (or its sacreBLEU variant), because word choice and order must match the reference closely (precision with n-grams). Reporting both for either task is common, but each metric matches one task's main concern.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0181","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-038-10-evaluation-metrics-rouge-bleu","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s029","q":"What happens to the <code>datasets.load_metric</code> call in this snippet in newer library versions?","a":"<p><code>load_metric</code> was deprecated in the Hugging Face <code>datasets</code> 2.x series and removed in <code>datasets</code> 3.0. Current code uses the separate <code>evaluate</code> library: <code>import evaluate; rouge = evaluate.load(\"rouge\")</code>, then <code>rouge.compute(predictions=..., references=...)</code>. The rouge metric also needs the <code>rouge_score</code> package installed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from datasets import load_metric\n\nrouge = load_metric(&quot;rouge&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0182","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-039-10-evaluation-metrics-rouge-bleu","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s029","q":"A model outputs \"The cat sat on the mat.\" and the reference is \"The cat is sitting on the mat.\" What are unigram recall and precision?","a":"<p>Output tokens: the, cat, sat, on, the, mat (6). Reference tokens: the, cat, is, sitting, on, the, mat (7). Matching with clipped counts, \"the\" matches twice, so the overlap is the(2) + cat + on + mat = 5. Recall (ROUGE-1 style) = 5/7, about 0.71. Precision (BLEU-1 style) = 5/6, about 0.83.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0183","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-039-10-evaluation-metrics-rouge-bleu","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s029","q":"What is wrong with computing unigram overlap with Python sets, as in this skeleton?","a":"<p>A set discards repeated words, so a word that appears twice in both texts is counted once and the overlap is too low. Real ROUGE and BLEU count n-gram occurrences with clipping: each word can match at most as many times as it appears in the reference. Use <code>collections.Counter</code> intersection (<code>Counter(a) &amp; Counter(b)</code>) or a library. Real implementations also normalise and tokenise punctuation, which <code>split()</code> does not.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def rouge_1(hyp, ref):\n    hyp_words = hyp.lower().split()\n    ref_words = ref.lower().split()\n    overlap = len(set(hyp_words) &amp; set(ref_words))\n    return overlap / len(ref_words)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0184","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-039-10-evaluation-metrics-rouge-bleu","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s029","q":"Why does BLEU combine its 1- to 4-gram precisions with a geometric mean and not an arithmetic mean?","a":"<p>The geometric mean forces the output to be good at every granularity: single words (adequacy) and longer phrases (fluency and word order). An arithmetic mean would let a high unigram precision hide a 4-gram precision near zero. The price is that one zero precision makes the whole score zero.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0185","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-039-10-evaluation-metrics-rouge-bleu","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s029","q":"How would you handle several valid reference translations when scoring with BLEU?","a":"<p>BLEU supports multiple references: each hypothesis n-gram is matched against all references, with clipping per n-gram taken from the reference that contains it most often. More references raise the score and reflect that many translations are correct. In NLTK, <code>sentence_bleu</code> takes a list of tokenised references.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0186","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-040-10-1-the-notebook-scorer-rouge-score-nlt","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s030","q":"What does <code>use_stemmer=True</code> change in <code>rouge_scorer.RougeScorer</code>?","a":"<p>It applies the Porter stemmer to tokens before matching, so \"running\" and \"runs\" both reduce to \"run\" and count as a match. It makes ROUGE less sensitive to inflection, so scores are slightly higher. Keep the setting the same across runs, or scores are not comparable. The scorer works on English text only.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>rouge = rouge_scorer.RougeScorer([&#x27;rouge1&#x27;, &#x27;rouge2&#x27;, &#x27;rougeL&#x27;], use_stemmer=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0187","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-040-10-1-the-notebook-scorer-rouge-score-nlt","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s030","q":"Why does this BLEU call pass <code>SmoothingFunction().method4</code>?","a":"<p>Sentence-level BLEU is zero whenever any n-gram order has no match, which is common for short sentences, and NLTK also warns about it. Smoothing replaces the zero counts with small values so the score is informative. There are several smoothing methods and they give different numbers, so state which one you use.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>bleu_smoothing = SmoothingFunction().method4\nbleu_score = sentence_bleu(reference_tokens, hypothesis_tokens, smoothing_function=bleu_smoothing)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0188","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-040-10-1-the-notebook-scorer-rouge-score-nlt","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s030","q":"In NLTK's <code>sentence_bleu</code>, why is the reference wrapped in a list while the hypothesis is not?","a":"<p>The first argument of <code>sentence_bleu</code> is a list of references, each one a list of tokens, because BLEU supports several references per sentence. The hypothesis is a single token list. Passing a bare token list for the reference would make NLTK treat each word as a separate reference.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>reference_tokens = [nltk.word_tokenize(reference)]\nhypothesis_tokens = nltk.word_tokenize(hypothesis)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0189","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-040-10-1-the-notebook-scorer-rouge-score-nlt","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s030","q":"Why average sentence-level BLEU scores, and is that the best way to evaluate a translation system?","a":"<p>Averaging sentence BLEU is simple, but the result is noisy and biased by short sentences that score zero or near zero. The standard approach is corpus-level BLEU, which pools n-gram counts across the whole test set before computing precision, ideally with a standard tool such as sacreBLEU so that tokenisation is fixed and scores can be compared between papers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0190","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-040-10-1-the-notebook-scorer-rouge-score-nlt","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s030","q":"Why does <code>word_tokenize</code> need <code>nltk.download('punkt')</code>, and what changed in newer NLTK versions?","a":"<p><code>word_tokenize</code> relies on the pre-trained Punkt sentence tokenizer data, which is not bundled with the package. In NLTK 3.8.2 and later (3.9 line), the required resource was renamed to <code>punkt_tab</code>, so recent versions need <code>nltk.download('punkt_tab')</code>. If you get a LookupError for a tokenizer resource, download the one named in the error message.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0191","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-040-10-1-the-notebook-scorer-rouge-score-nlt","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s030","q":"How do you interpret a BLEU score such as 0.12, and what scale is it on?","a":"<p>BLEU ranges from 0 to 1 (many tools print it as 0 to 100). A rough reading: over 0.3 is good, 0.2 to 0.3 is understandable, and under 0.1 is poor. It depends on language pair, domain, number of references and tokenisation, so use it to compare systems on the same test set, not as an absolute quality grade. A low-resource or morphologically rich target such as German typically scores lower than French.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0192","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-041-11-challenge-wmt16-english-german-transf","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s031","q":"In this preprocessing, why does every input start with a task prefix, and what changes if you want German to English?","a":"<p>T5-family models are text-to-text: the prefix tells the model which task to run, and fine-tuning teaches it the mapping for that prefix. For the reverse direction you swap the fields (<code>de</code> as input, <code>en</code> as target) and change the prefix, for example \"translate German to English:\". Use the same prefix at training and inference.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inputs = [f&#x27;Translate English to German: {example[&quot;en&quot;]}&#x27; for example in examples[&#x27;translation&#x27;]]\ntargets = [example[&#x27;de&#x27;] for example in examples[&#x27;translation&#x27;]]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0193","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-041-11-challenge-wmt16-english-german-transf","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s031","q":"How is a Hugging Face translation dataset such as WMT16 structured, and how do you access the two languages?","a":"<p>Each row has a <code>translation</code> field holding a dictionary with one key per language, for example <code>{'en': ..., 'de': ...}</code>. The config name (such as <code>de-en</code>) selects the language pair and the splits are <code>train</code>, <code>validation</code> and <code>test</code>. Preprocessing functions that expect a <code>translation</code> field fail on datasets with a different schema.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0194","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-041-11-challenge-wmt16-english-german-transf","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s031","q":"What is wrong with evaluating a translation model on only the first batch of the test set, as this function does?","a":"<p>One batch is a small and, if the loader shuffles, random sample, so the score is noisy and not reproducible. Iterate over the whole test set (or a fixed, large enough sample), keep the order fixed, and use corpus-level BLEU. Also, the test set must not have been used to choose hyperparameters, or the score is optimistic.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def translate_and_evaluate(dataset):\n    bleu_scores = []\n    batch = next(iter(dataset))\n    ...\n    return sum(bleu_scores) / len(bleu_scores)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0195","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-041-11-challenge-wmt16-english-german-transf","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s031","q":"How would you set up fine-tuning of an English-to-German translation model if you can only afford to train for a few minutes on one GPU?","a":"<p>Start from a pre-trained multilingual or instruction-tuned checkpoint (T5 or FLAN-T5 base), train on a subset of tens of thousands of sentence pairs, freeze the encoder, decoder and embeddings and train the head, or use LoRA. Use a small learning rate for full fine-tuning, a higher one for new or adapter weights. Evaluate with BLEU on a fixed test slice and compare with the untouched model as a baseline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0196","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-042-11-challenge-wmt16-english-german-transf","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s031","q":"The BLEU score after a short run with a frozen encoder and decoder is low. What would you try to improve it?","a":"<p>In order of cost: check the data and tokenisation (truncation, padding, prefix); train longer and watch validation loss; use more data; unfreeze the last decoder or encoder layers, or switch to LoRA on attention layers so that more of the network can adapt; use a larger base model; use beam search with a sensible length; and compare with the zero-shot baseline to see how much fine-tuning actually adds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0197","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-043-12-1-peft-in-one-view","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s032","q":"What is parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>PEFT adapts a large pre-trained model by training only a small number of parameters (often well under 1 percent), either by adding small modules or by learning small updates, while the base weights stay frozen. It solves the cost of full fine-tuning: huge GPU memory for gradients and optimiser states, long training times and a full model copy for every task.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0198","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-043-12-1-peft-in-one-view","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s032","q":"Compare full fine-tuning, transfer learning with a new head, and PEFT.","a":"<p>Full fine-tuning updates all weights: best quality ceiling, highest compute, one full model copy per task. Transfer learning with a new head trains new layers and perhaps a few base layers: cheaper but limited to what the frozen features allow. PEFT trains small adapter-style modules: low memory and fast training, and storage per task is only the small adapter file while the base model is shared.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0199","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-043-12-1-peft-in-one-view","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s032","q":"Why does PEFT cut GPU memory use during training even though the forward pass still goes through the whole model?","a":"<p>Gradients and optimiser state (Adam keeps two extra values per parameter) are only needed for trainable parameters. With 0.1 to 1 percent trainable, those two items shrink by about the same factor. Weights and activations still need memory, but activation memory can be handled with gradient checkpointing and smaller batches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0200","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-043-12-1-peft-in-one-view","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s032","q":"Name common PEFT methods and how they differ.","a":"<p>Adapters insert small bottleneck layers into each transformer block. LoRA adds trainable low-rank matrices to existing weight matrices. Prefix tuning and prompt tuning learn virtual token embeddings (or per-layer key/value prefixes) that are prepended to the input. IA3 learns small vectors that rescale activations. QLoRA is LoRA applied on a base model quantised to 4 bits. LoRA is the most widely used because it adds no inference latency once merged.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0201","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-043-12-1-peft-in-one-view","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s032","q":"When would you not choose PEFT, and what are its drawbacks?","a":"<p>For a large domain shift with plenty of data and compute, full fine-tuning may reach higher quality, because adapters have limited capacity. PEFT also adds hyperparameters (rank, alpha, target modules) and unmerged adapters can add a little inference latency. Quality close to full fine-tuning is typical, but not guaranteed, so compare against a baseline on your task.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0202","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-043-12-1-peft-in-one-view","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s032","q":"You must serve ten tasks (summarisation, translation, classification and so on) from one base model. How does PEFT help?","a":"<p>Keep one copy of the base model and train one small adapter per task. Store each adapter as a file of a few megabytes, load the right one for each request (or merge it per deployment), and add tasks without retraining or duplicating the base. Without PEFT you would store and serve ten full models.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0203","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-044-12-2-adapters-the-idea-lora-refines","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s033","q":"How does a bottleneck adapter work in a transformer layer?","a":"<p>It projects the hidden state down to a small width (<code>adapter_dim</code>), applies a non-linearity, projects back up, and adds the result to the input. Only these small layers are trained, and the base model stays frozen. Parameters per adapter are about 2 x input_dim x adapter_dim.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import torch.nn as nn\n\nclass Adapter(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, input_dim, adapter_dim=64):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.down_project = nn.Linear(input_dim, adapter_dim)\n        self.activation = nn.ReLU()\n        self.up_project = nn.Linear(adapter_dim, input_dim)\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return x + self.up_project(self.activation(self.down_project(x)))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0204","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-044-12-2-adapters-the-idea-lora-refines","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s033","q":"Why does the adapter's <code>forward</code> return <code>x + ...</code> instead of only the adapter output?","a":"<p>The residual connection means the adapter learns a correction to the hidden state, not a new one. If the adapter output is near zero (small initial weights), the model behaves like the pre-trained one at the start of training, which keeps training stable and preserves existing knowledge.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0205","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-044-12-2-adapters-the-idea-lora-refines","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s033","q":"What does the <code>adapter_dim</code> (bottleneck width) control?","a":"<p>It sets adapter capacity and size. A smaller bottleneck means fewer trainable parameters and less capacity to learn the task. A larger one adds capacity and cost. It plays the same role as the rank in LoRA.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0206","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-044-12-2-adapters-the-idea-lora-refines","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s033","q":"What is the main difference between adapters and LoRA at inference time?","a":"<p>Adapter layers sit in series with the model's own layers, so they add extra computation to every forward pass and cannot be folded into existing weights. LoRA's update is a low-rank matrix added to a weight matrix, so after training it can be merged (W plus the scaled product of the two matrices) and the model runs at its original speed. This is a major reason LoRA replaced adapters in many setups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0207","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-044-12-2-adapters-the-idea-lora-refines","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s033","q":"Where are adapters usually inserted in a transformer block?","a":"<p>After the attention sub-layer and/or after the feed-forward sub-layer, before the residual and layer normalisation of that sub-layer. Original adapter designs placed two per block; later work showed one per block is often enough.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0208","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-045-12-3-lora-low-rank-adaptation-the-math","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s034","q":"What is LoRA and how does it reduce trainable parameters?","a":"<p>LoRA freezes the pre-trained weight matrix W and learns its update as a product of two small matrices, delta W = B x A, with inner dimension r (the rank). For a d x d matrix that is 2 x d x r trainable numbers instead of d squared. For d = 512 and r = 8 that is 8,192 instead of 262,144, about 32 times fewer. The layer output becomes W x + (alpha / r) x B A x.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0209","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-045-12-3-lora-low-rank-adaptation-the-math","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s034","q":"Why does a low-rank update work for fine-tuning?","a":"<p>The hypothesis behind LoRA is that the change needed to adapt a pre-trained model to a task has a low intrinsic rank: the useful update lives in a small subspace of the full weight space. So a low-rank product can approximate it well. Empirically small ranks (4 to 16) often match full fine-tuning on many tasks, though harder tasks may need more.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0210","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-045-12-3-lora-low-rank-adaptation-the-math","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s034","q":"Why is one of the two LoRA matrices initialised to zeros?","a":"<p>With B equal to zero, the product B A is zero, so at step 0 the adapted layer gives exactly the pre-trained output and training starts from the known good model. A is random so gradients flow to B (if both were zero, there would be no gradient signal). B then grows during training.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>self.lora_A = nn.Parameter(torch.randn(rank, in_dim) * 0.01)\nself.lora_B = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(out_dim, rank))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0211","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-045-12-3-lora-low-rank-adaptation-the-math","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s034","q":"What does the <code>alpha / rank</code> factor do in this forward pass?","a":"<p>It scales the LoRA update. Dividing by rank keeps the update's size similar when you change the rank, so the learning rate needs less retuning. <code>alpha</code> is a hyperparameter that sets how strongly the adapter influences the output. Doubling alpha at fixed rank is roughly like increasing the learning rate for the adapter.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>original_output = self.original_layer(x)\nlora_output = (x @ self.lora_A.T @ self.lora_B.T) * (self.alpha / self.rank)\nreturn original_output + lora_output</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0212","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-045-12-3-lora-low-rank-adaptation-the-math","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s034","q":"In this LoRA layer, why does the code set <code>requires_grad = False</code> on the original layer's parameters?","a":"<p>It excludes the base weights from gradient computation and the optimiser, so only <code>lora_A</code> and <code>lora_B</code> are updated. This saves memory for gradients and optimiser state and keeps the pre-trained weights intact, so the same base can be reused for other tasks.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for param in self.original_layer.parameters():\n    param.requires_grad = False</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0213","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-045-12-3-lora-low-rank-adaptation-the-math","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s034","q":"Why does LoRA add no inference latency after training?","a":"<p>The low-rank update can be merged into the weights: W' = W + (alpha / r) x B A. The merged model has exactly the original architecture and cost. You can also keep the adapter separate to swap tasks, at the price of a small extra computation per layer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0214","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-045-12-3-lora-low-rank-adaptation-the-math","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s034","q":"What are the limitations of LoRA?","a":"<p>Capacity is limited by the rank, so tasks needing a large behavioural change can lag full fine-tuning. It adds hyperparameters (rank, alpha, which modules). It does not reduce the memory needed to hold the base model or activations (QLoRA addresses base weight memory by quantising it). Unmerged adapters add some latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0215","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-046-12-4-lora-with-the-hugging-face-peft-lib","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s035","q":"What does each argument of this <code>LoraConfig</code> do?","a":"<p><code>task_type</code> tells peft which model type this is, so it wraps the model correctly (sequence-to-sequence language model). <code>inference_mode=False</code> means the adapter is for training, so its weights are trainable. <code>r</code> is the rank, <code>lora_alpha</code> scales the update (by alpha / r), <code>lora_dropout</code> applies dropout on the adapter input for regularisation, and <code>target_modules</code> lists the layers (by name) that get adapters.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>lora_config = LoraConfig(\n    task_type=TaskType.SEQ_2_SEQ_LM,\n    inference_mode=False,\n    r=8,  # Rank\n    lora_alpha=32,  # Scaling parameter\n    lora_dropout=0.1,\n    target_modules=[&quot;q&quot;, &quot;v&quot;]  # Target attention layers\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0216","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-046-12-4-lora-with-the-hugging-face-peft-lib","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s035","q":"What does <code>get_peft_model(base_model, lora_config)</code> return and what does it change?","a":"<p>It returns a <code>PeftModel</code> that wraps the base model, injects LoRA layers into the matching modules, freezes the base weights and leaves only the adapter weights trainable. It modifies the model's modules in place, so the original base object is also changed. <code>print_trainable_parameters()</code> shows the trainable count and its percentage of the total.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0217","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-046-12-4-lora-with-the-hugging-face-peft-lib","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s035","q":"Why must <code>target_modules</code> use different names for different model families?","a":"<p>The names are the sub-module names inside the model code. T5 calls its attention projections <code>q</code>, <code>k</code>, <code>v</code>, <code>o</code>, while Llama-style models use <code>q_proj</code>, <code>k_proj</code>, <code>v_proj</code>, <code>o_proj</code> and MLP layers <code>gate_proj</code>, <code>up_proj</code>, <code>down_proj</code>. A wrong name matches nothing, and peft raises an error. Print <code>model</code> or <code>named_modules()</code> to see the real names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0218","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-046-12-4-lora-with-the-hugging-face-peft-lib","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s035","q":"Which layers should receive LoRA adapters, and why are <code>q</code> and <code>v</code> a common default?","a":"<p>The original LoRA paper adapted the attention query and value projections and got good results with few parameters. Adding more modules (all attention projections, MLP layers) raises capacity and cost, and many recent recipes target all linear layers for better quality. Start small, then widen the targets if validation quality is too low.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0219","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-046-12-4-lora-with-the-hugging-face-peft-lib","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s035","q":"What does <code>task_type</code> change in <code>LoraConfig</code>?","a":"<p>It selects the peft wrapper class for the task, such as <code>SEQ_2_SEQ_LM</code> (encoder-decoder like T5), <code>CAUSAL_LM</code> (decoder-only like GPT or Llama) or <code>SEQ_CLS</code> (classification). The wrapper handles the correct forward arguments and, for classification, keeps the new head trainable. Choosing the wrong one gives errors or wrong behaviour.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0220","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-046-12-4-lora-with-the-hugging-face-peft-lib","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s035","q":"Why are LoRA training learning rates usually higher than in full fine-tuning?","a":"<p>The adapter weights start near zero and are few, so they need larger steps to learn, and there is less risk of damaging the base weights because those are frozen. 1e-4 to 3e-4 is typical for LoRA, compared with 1e-5 to 5e-5 for full fine-tuning. Tune it on validation loss.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0221","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-047-12-4-lora-with-the-hugging-face-peft-lib","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s035","q":"In this loop, the optimiser receives <code>model.parameters()</code>. Does it update the frozen base weights?","a":"<p>No. Frozen parameters have <code>requires_grad=False</code>, so they get no gradient, and PyTorch optimisers skip parameters whose gradient is <code>None</code>. Only the LoRA weights change. Passing only the trainable ones, <code>filter(lambda p: p.requires_grad, model.parameters())</code>, is also common and makes the intent clear.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>optimizer = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-4)\n...\nloss.backward()\noptimizer.step()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0222","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-047-12-4-lora-with-the-hugging-face-peft-lib","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s035","q":"Why must <code>optimizer.zero_grad()</code> come before <code>loss.backward()</code> in each step of a PyTorch training loop?","a":"<p>PyTorch accumulates gradients into <code>.grad</code> on every backward call. Without clearing them, gradients from the previous batch add to the current ones and the update is wrong. Clearing before <code>backward()</code> (or right after <code>step()</code>) gives each batch its own gradients. Deliberate accumulation is used on purpose for gradient accumulation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0223","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-047-12-4-lora-with-the-hugging-face-peft-lib","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s035","q":"What pitfall does this inference helper have for a model that was just trained?","a":"<p>It never calls <code>model.eval()</code>. After training the model is still in train mode, so dropout (including <code>lora_dropout</code>) stays active and the output is random and worse. Call <code>model.eval()</code> before inference. <code>torch.no_grad()</code> only stops gradient tracking and does not change dropout.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def peft_inference(model, tokenizer, input_text, task_type=&quot;translation&quot;):\n    ...\n    with torch.no_grad():\n        outputs = model.generate(**inputs, max_length=150, num_beams=4, early_stopping=True)\n    ...</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0224","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-047-12-4-lora-with-the-hugging-face-peft-lib","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s035","q":"How do you save and reload a trained LoRA model with peft?","a":"<p><code>peft_model.save_pretrained(\"path\")</code> saves only the adapter weights and config (a few megabytes), not the base model. To reload, load the base model and call <code>PeftModel.from_pretrained(base_model, \"path\")</code>. To remove the adapter overhead for deployment, call <code>merge_and_unload()</code> and get a plain model with the update folded into the weights.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0225","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-047-12-4-lora-with-the-hugging-face-peft-lib","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s035","q":"What is <code>merge_and_unload()</code> in peft, and when would you not merge?","a":"<p>It adds the scaled LoRA product into the base weights and removes the adapter modules, returning a standard model that runs at base speed and can be saved as one file. Do not merge if you need to switch adapters per request or keep the adapter small and separate. Merging into a quantised base needs extra care because of rounding.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0226","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-048-12-5-one-base-model-many-task-adapters","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s036","q":"What is the problem with calling <code>get_peft_model</code> several times on the same base model, as in this class?","a":"<p><code>get_peft_model</code> injects adapter layers into the base model in place, so repeated calls stack or overwrite adapters in the same object, and the stored \"separate\" models share one base. Load the base once and use peft's multi-adapter API (<code>add_adapter</code>, <code>load_adapter</code>, <code>set_adapter</code>), or load a fresh base per adapter.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def add_task_adapter(self, task_name, lora_config):\n    adapter_model = get_peft_model(self.base_model, lora_config)\n    self.task_adapters[task_name] = adapter_model\n    return adapter_model</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0227","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-048-12-5-one-base-model-many-task-adapters","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s036","q":"How do you serve several task-specific LoRA adapters on one base model with peft?","a":"<p>Load the base model once, attach the first adapter with <code>PeftModel.from_pretrained(base, path, adapter_name=\"summarise\")</code>, add others with <code>model.load_adapter(path, adapter_name=\"translate\")</code>, and choose the active one with <code>model.set_adapter(\"translate\")</code>. Route each request to the adapter for its task. Serving systems such as vLLM can also batch requests that use different LoRA adapters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0228","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-048-12-5-one-base-model-many-task-adapters","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s036","q":"What are the storage and deployment benefits of per-task adapters?","a":"<p>Each adapter is typically megabytes against gigabytes for a full model. Ten tasks need one base plus ten small files. Updating or rolling back one task means replacing one small file, and adapters can be versioned separately from the base model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0229","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-048-12-5-one-base-model-many-task-adapters","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s036","q":"How would you measure how parameter-efficient a PEFT setup is?","a":"<p>Divide trainable by total parameters. <code>print_trainable_parameters()</code> reports the same. Typical LoRA values are 0.1 to 1 percent. The count of trainable parameters also predicts the adapter file size and the optimiser memory.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>trainable = sum(p.numel() for p in peft_model.parameters() if p.requires_grad)\ntotal = sum(p.numel() for p in peft_model.parameters())\nratio = trainable / total</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0230","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-049-12-5-one-base-model-many-task-adapters","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s036","q":"What does it mean for a LoRA rank to be adaptive, and why would you want it?","a":"<p>Instead of one fixed rank for every layer, the effective rank is adjusted (for example by training with a maximum rank and using only the first rows, or by pruning unimportant ranks during training, as AdaLoRA does). Layers differ in how much they need to change, so budget is spent where it helps, giving the same quality with fewer parameters. The cost is extra complexity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0231","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-049-12-5-one-base-model-many-task-adapters","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s036","q":"In this adaptive layer, why are only the first <code>current_rank</code> rows of <code>lora_A</code> and columns of <code>lora_B</code> used in the forward pass?","a":"<p>The layer allocates matrices for the maximum rank, and slicing makes smaller ranks a subset of the larger one. The rank can then be changed during training without reallocating weights. Rows beyond <code>current_rank</code> get no gradient while inactive.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>A_active = self.lora_A[:self.current_rank, :]\nB_active = self.lora_B[:, :self.current_rank]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0232","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-049-12-5-one-base-model-many-task-adapters","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s036","q":"How would you decide between training one adapter on a mix of tasks and one adapter per task?","a":"<p>One adapter per task gives the best quality per task, easy rollback and no interference, at the cost of routing and more files. A single multi-task adapter is simpler to serve and can share knowledge between related tasks, but tasks can interfere and balancing the data mix is harder. Start per task, and merge tasks only if serving complexity requires it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0233","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-050-12-6-lora-peft-guidelines-lecture-as-a-t","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s037","q":"What starting values would you use for LoRA rank, alpha, dropout and learning rate on a new task?","a":"<p>Rank 8 (use 4 for small tasks, 16 to 64 for complex ones), alpha equal to 2 x rank (a common heuristic, 16 for rank 8) or equal to rank, dropout 0.05 to 0.1, and a learning rate around 1e-4 to 3e-4. Then adjust from validation results. These are heuristics, not rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0234","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-050-12-6-lora-peft-guidelines-lecture-as-a-t","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s037","q":"How much data do you need to fine-tune with LoRA?","a":"<p>A few hundred high-quality examples can give a clear gain for a narrow task, and one to ten thousand is typical for solid results. Quality and diversity beat volume: noisy or duplicated data hurts more than a small clean set. Hold out a validation set from the start.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0235","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-050-12-6-lora-peft-guidelines-lecture-as-a-t","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s037","q":"How many epochs and what batch size would you start with for LoRA?","a":"<p>Start with 3 epochs and a batch size of 16 to 32 as memory allows (use gradient accumulation to reach a larger effective batch). Stop earlier if validation loss rises. Small datasets overfit after a few epochs, so monitor validation loss every epoch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0236","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-050-12-6-lora-peft-guidelines-lecture-as-a-t","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s037","q":"Why should you measure inference latency, not only accuracy, when you choose a LoRA setup?","a":"<p>A higher rank, more target modules and an unmerged adapter add computation, and generation latency drives user experience and cost. Measure average and tail latency on representative prompts, with the same batch size and decoding settings as in production. Merging the adapter removes most of the overhead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0237","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-051-13-1-overfitting-vs-generalisability","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s038","q":"How do you recognise overfitting when fine-tuning with LoRA, and what do you change?","a":"<p>Training loss keeps falling while validation loss stalls or rises. Fix by lowering the rank, adding or raising dropout and weight decay, stopping earlier with early stopping, adding more or more varied data, or lowering the learning rate. Keep the checkpoint with the best validation score.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0238","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-051-13-1-overfitting-vs-generalisability","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s038","q":"Why can fine-tuned models lose generality, and how do you reduce this?","a":"<p>Training on a narrow dataset shifts the weights toward that distribution (catastrophic forgetting) and can lower performance on other tasks. LoRA reduces the risk because the base weights are frozen, but a high rank or learning rate can still over-specialise. Mix some general data into training, keep ranks small, evaluate on a general benchmark as well as the target task, and keep the base model available for other uses.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0239","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-052-13-2-choosing-the-rank","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s039","q":"How do you choose the LoRA rank for a task?","a":"<p>Start low (4 to 8), train, and measure on a validation set. Increase the rank (4, 8, 16, 32) while validation quality improves meaningfully, and stop when gains flatten or overfitting appears. Pick the smallest rank that gets close to the best score, since it is cheaper to train, store and serve.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0240","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-052-13-2-choosing-the-rank","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s039","q":"Why is a higher LoRA rank not always better?","a":"<p>A higher rank adds trainable parameters and capacity, which raises the risk of overfitting on small datasets and adds cost. If the needed update is low-rank, extra rank learns noise. Gains from rank usually flatten quickly, so very high ranks rarely pay off.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0241","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-052-13-2-choosing-the-rank","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s039","q":"How many trainable parameters does a LoRA of rank r add to one d x d matrix, and how much memory is that in float32?","a":"<p>Two matrices (d x r and r x d), so 2 x d x r parameters. In float32 each uses 4 bytes. For d = 512 and r = 8 that is 8,192 parameters, about 0.03 MB per adapted matrix. Multiply by the number of adapted matrices, and add optimiser state (about 2 more values per parameter with Adam) for training.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>params = 2 * base_dim * rank\nmemory_mb = params * 4 / 1e6</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0242","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-052-13-2-choosing-the-rank","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s039","q":"What is the idea of a progressive rank search that doubles the rank each time?","a":"<p>Try ranks 1, 2, 4, 8, ... and evaluate each on validation data. Stop when the score no longer improves, or drops (for example to under 95 percent of the best). Doubling covers the range with few training runs because the effect of rank is roughly logarithmic.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>current_rank = start_rank\nwhile current_rank &lt;= max_rank:\n    ...\n    current_rank *= 2</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0243","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-053-13-2-choosing-the-rank","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s039","q":"What is an adaptive rank policy and what risks does it have?","a":"<p>The rank is raised when recent validation performance stays under a target and lowered when it is well over it, so capacity follows the need. Risks: changing rank mid-training changes the parameter shapes (optimiser state must be rebuilt), results are harder to reproduce, and noisy metrics can cause rank flapping. Most teams run a fixed-rank search before the final run instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0244","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-053-13-2-choosing-the-rank","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s039","q":"What does it mean when ratio rank / base_dim is very small, and why does it still work?","a":"<p>A small ratio means the update is a very compressed approximation of a full matrix. It works because task adaptation needs a low-dimensional change to the weights. If quality is too low, raise the rank; if the model overfits, lower it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0245","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-053-13-2-choosing-the-rank","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s039","q":"Does the best LoRA rank depend on model size and task?","a":"<p>Yes. Tasks close to pre-training (style, format, simple classification) need low ranks. Tasks that teach new knowledge or a very different domain need more capacity. Larger models often reach good results at low ranks. Always tune on validation data, because published best ranks do not transfer between tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0246","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-054-13-3-learning-rate-batch-size-epochs","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s040","q":"How does a learning-rate range test work?","a":"<p>Train for a few steps at each learning rate from a range spaced exponentially (small to large), and record the loss. Pick the rate in the region where loss falls fastest, and use something conservative (often a tenth of the steepest point). It is a quick way to find the usable range without full runs. Use a fresh model copy for each trial.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>lrs = np.logspace(np.log10(lr_range[0]), np.log10(lr_range[1]), 20)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0247","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-054-13-3-learning-rate-batch-size-epochs","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s040","q":"Why is <code>np.logspace</code> used for the learning rate range instead of a linear range?","a":"<p>Learning rates matter by order of magnitude: 1e-5 and 2e-5 behave similarly while 1e-5 and 1e-3 differ greatly. A log scale gives equal coverage to each order of magnitude, while a linear grid from 1e-5 to 1e-2 would put nearly all points near the top.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0248","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-054-13-3-learning-rate-batch-size-epochs","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s040","q":"What does batch size change in training, and how do you choose it?","a":"<p>A larger batch gives smoother gradient estimates and fewer steps per epoch, which is faster per epoch but needs more memory and can generalise slightly worse. A small batch is noisier and may be unstable but adds regularisation. Pick the largest that fits in memory (often 16 to 64), and use gradient accumulation to reach a larger effective batch. If you raise the batch size, you often raise the learning rate too.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0249","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-054-13-3-learning-rate-batch-size-epochs","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s040","q":"How do you pick the number of epochs for fine-tuning?","a":"<p>Do not fix it in advance: train with validation after each epoch and stop when validation loss stops improving (early stopping), keeping the best checkpoint. For small datasets 3 to 5 epochs is typical, and more epochs on a small set cause overfitting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0250","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-054-13-3-learning-rate-batch-size-epochs","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s040","q":"What are a warm-up and a learning-rate scheduler, and why use them?","a":"<p>Warm-up raises the learning rate from near zero to its target over the first few hundred steps, which avoids large unstable updates while optimiser statistics (Adam) are still poor. A scheduler (linear or cosine decay) then reduces the rate so that training settles. Both are standard in transformer fine-tuning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0251","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-055-13-4-cross-validation-regularisation-mon","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s041","q":"What is k-fold cross-validation and why is it used to pick the LoRA rank?","a":"<p>The data is split into k folds. For each candidate setting, the model trains k times, each time validating on a different fold, and the scores are averaged. The mean and standard deviation show whether a rank is better than another beyond noise. It is most useful with small datasets, where one validation split is unreliable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0252","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-055-13-4-cross-validation-regularisation-mon","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s041","q":"Why is full cross-validation rarely used when fine-tuning large language models?","a":"<p>Each fold is a full training run, so k folds times several candidates multiplies the cost, which is too high for large models. In practice you use a single train/validation split, sometimes with a few seeds, and use cross-validation only on small models or small datasets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0253","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-055-13-4-cross-validation-regularisation-mon","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s041","q":"Why use <code>KFold(shuffle=True, random_state=42)</code>?","a":"<p>Shuffling avoids folds that follow the data's order (for example sorted by class or date), and the fixed <code>random_state</code> makes the split reproducible, so results can be compared across runs. For classification with imbalanced classes, <code>StratifiedKFold</code> keeps class proportions in each fold.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0254","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-055-13-4-cross-validation-regularisation-mon","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s041","q":"What do L1 and L2 penalties do when applied to the LoRA matrices?","a":"<p>L2 (weight decay) shrinks the weights smoothly toward zero, limiting how far the adapter moves from the base model. L1 pushes many weights exactly to zero, giving sparse updates. Both reduce overfitting. In practice, AdamW's decoupled <code>weight_decay</code> argument is the usual way to apply L2 instead of adding the term to the loss.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>l1_loss += torch.sum(torch.abs(param))\nl2_loss += torch.sum(param ** 2)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0255","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-056-13-4-cross-validation-regularisation-mon","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s041","q":"What do <code>patience</code> and <code>min_delta</code> do in this monitor?","a":"<p><code>min_delta</code> is the smallest improvement that counts. Smaller gains are treated as no change, which ignores noise. <code>patience</code> is how many consecutive evaluations without a counted improvement are allowed before stopping. Together they stop training once progress has really stopped, and the best checkpoint should be restored.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class LoRATrainingMonitor:\n    def __init__(self, patience=5, min_delta=0.001):\n        ...\n        if val_score &gt; self.best_score + self.min_delta:\n            self.best_score = val_score\n            self.wait = 0</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0256","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-056-13-4-cross-validation-regularisation-mon","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s041","q":"Why monitor the validation score and not only training loss?","a":"<p>Training loss only shows how well the model fits data it has seen, and it keeps falling even when the model is memorising. Validation loss and a task metric (ROUGE, BLEU, accuracy) on held-out data show the generalisation that matters in production. A growing gap between train and validation loss is the classic sign of overfitting. Also track the learning rate and gradient norm for stability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0257","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-056-13-4-cross-validation-regularisation-mon","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s041","q":"What would you check if validation loss increases from the first epoch of a LoRA run?","a":"<p>A learning rate that is too high (try 3 to 10 times lower), too high a rank or too many epochs for a small set, a mismatch between train and validation data, wrong label masking or tokenisation, and data leakage or duplicates. Also check that the adapter is actually the only trainable part.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0258","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-056-13-4-cross-validation-regularisation-mon","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s041","q":"What are the common mistakes when tuning LoRA?","a":"<p>Using a rank that is too high for a small dataset, a learning rate that is too large, not monitoring validation metrics, no regularisation, stopping before convergence, reusing a modified base model between experiments, and judging by training loss alone.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0259","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-057-14-lora-in-tensorflow-implementation-exp","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s042","q":"Why do TensorFlow projects often write their own LoRA layer, and what would you use in PyTorch?","a":"<p>The Hugging Face <code>peft</code> library is built for PyTorch models and does not support the TensorFlow model classes, so a Keras project has to implement the low-rank layer itself. In PyTorch you would use <code>peft</code> with <code>LoraConfig</code> and <code>get_peft_model</code>, which handle injection, freezing and saving.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0260","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-057-14-lora-in-tensorflow-implementation-exp","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s042","q":"What are the steps to add LoRA to a pre-trained Keras model by hand?","a":"<p>Freeze all pre-trained weights. Write a layer that wraps a dense layer and adds <code>x @ A @ B</code>, with A and B trainable. Put it in place of the dense layers that should be adapted, so that the model's forward pass actually calls it. Check <code>model.summary()</code> that only the A and B weights are trainable. Compile with a suitable learning rate, train, and evaluate. Save only the adapter weights.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0261","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-058-14-1-data-prep-wmt16-de-en-20-000-train","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s043","q":"How do you compute the number of optimiser steps per epoch, and why does it matter for fine-tuning?","a":"<p>Steps per epoch = ceil(number of training examples / batch size), because the last batch may be smaller. Total steps = steps per epoch x epochs. A learning-rate schedule and its warm-up are defined in steps, so you need the total to set them correctly. A smaller batch means more steps per epoch, and training time estimates also come from this count.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0262","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-058-14-1-data-prep-wmt16-de-en-20-000-train","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s043","q":"What happens if a preprocessing function written for translation data (a <code>translation</code> field) runs on a different dataset?","a":"<p>It raises a <code>KeyError</code>, because the dataset has no <code>translation</code> column (a summarisation set has <code>article</code> and <code>highlights</code>, for example). Always check the dataset's columns (<code>dataset['train'].column_names</code>) and print one row before writing the preprocessing.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inputs = [f&#x27;Translate English to German: {example[&quot;en&quot;]}&#x27; for example in examples[&#x27;translation&#x27;]]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0263","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-058-14-1-data-prep-wmt16-de-en-20-000-train","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s043","q":"Why is <code>tensorflow_addons</code> a risky dependency for a new project?","a":"<p>The TensorFlow Addons package reached end of life in May 2024 and no longer receives updates, and it can conflict with newer TensorFlow and Keras versions. Prefer features in core Keras or other maintained libraries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0264","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-059-14-2-the-keras-loralayer","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s044","q":"In this Keras layer, why are the weights created in <code>build</code> and not in <code>__init__</code>?","a":"<p>The input dimension is only known when the layer first sees data. Keras calls <code>build</code> once with the input shape, so the weight shape <code>(input_dim, rank)</code> can be created then. <code>add_weight</code> also registers the variable so that it is tracked as trainable.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def build(self, input_shape):\n    self.w_a = self.add_weight(shape=(input_shape[-1], self.rank),\n                               initializer=&#x27;random_normal&#x27;,\n                               trainable=True, name=&#x27;w_a&#x27;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0265","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-059-14-2-the-keras-loralayer","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s044","q":"What does the forward pass of this layer compute?","a":"<p>It computes the wrapped layer's normal output plus a low-rank correction: <code>dense(x) + (x @ A) @ B</code>. A has shape (input_dim, rank) and B has shape (rank, units), so the correction has the same shape as the dense output. Multiplying x by A first keeps the intermediate small.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def call(self, inputs):\n    original_output = self.dense(inputs)\n    lora_output = tf.matmul(tf.matmul(inputs, self.w_a), self.w_b)\n    self.dense.trainable = False\n    return original_output + lora_output</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0266","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-059-14-2-the-keras-loralayer","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s044","q":"What is questionable about initialising both LoRA matrices with <code>random_normal</code> and not scaling the update?","a":"<p>Standard LoRA initialises one matrix to zero, so the adapted model starts exactly as the pre-trained one. With both random, the correction is non-zero at step 0 and perturbs the pre-trained outputs, which can hurt early training. The usual recipe also scales by alpha / rank. Initialise B with zeros (<code>initializer='zeros'</code>) and add the scale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0267","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-059-14-2-the-keras-loralayer","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s044","q":"Why is <code>self.dense.trainable = False</code> in <code>call</code> a poor place to freeze the layer?","a":"<p>Trainable flags should be set when the layer is built or wrapped, before compiling, because Keras collects trainable weights when the model is compiled and built. Setting it during a forward pass is fragile and runs on every call. Set it in <code>__init__</code>, and verify with <code>model.summary()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0268","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-060-14-2-the-keras-loralayer","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s044","q":"In a Keras model, why does this loop not insert LoRA layers into the model?","a":"<p><code>layer = LoRALayer(layer)</code> only rebinds the loop variable. The model still holds the original layer objects, so the loop only freezes layers. Assigning into the list <code>model.layers[i] = LoRALayer(...)</code> fails for the same reason: <code>model.layers</code> is just a list of the model's layers, and changing it does not rewire the computation in the model's <code>call</code>. To adapt a layer, replace the attribute that the forward code calls (or build the model with the wrapper from the start), then confirm that <code>w_a</code> and <code>w_b</code> appear among the trainable weights.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for ix, layer in enumerate(model.decoder._flatten_layers()):\n    if isinstance(layer, tf_keras.src.layers.core.dense.Dense):\n        layer.trainable = False\n        layer = LoRALayer(layer)\n    else:\n        layer.trainable = False</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0269","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-060-14-2-the-keras-loralayer","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s044","q":"How can you check that LoRA is really applied and only the adapter trains?","a":"<p>Compare <code>model.summary()</code> or the counts of <code>trainable_weights</code> and <code>non_trainable_weights</code> before and after. The trainable count should equal the adapter parameters (2 x d x r per wrapped layer), and the names <code>w_a</code> and <code>w_b</code> should appear in <code>model.trainable_weights</code>. If the trainable count equals the count from a plain freeze, the adapter is not in the computation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0270","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-061-14-3-compile-train-evaluate","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s045","q":"Why do Hugging Face TensorFlow models use an optimiser from <code>tf_keras</code> in this setup?","a":"<p>With TensorFlow 2.16 and later, <code>tf.keras</code> is Keras 3, but Hugging Face TensorFlow models are built on Keras 2. The <code>tf_keras</code> package provides Keras 2, and Hugging Face recommends it for these models. Mixing Keras 3 optimisers and layers with them can fail. (In recent transformers releases, TensorFlow support is in maintenance mode, and PyTorch is the main path.)</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0271","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-061-14-3-compile-train-evaluate","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s045","q":"What does <code>model.evaluate(test_dataset)</code> return and how would you interpret it?","a":"<p>It runs the model over the dataset without updating weights and returns the loss (and any compiled metrics). For seq2seq training the loss is token-level cross-entropy, so lower is better. It is not a translation-quality score: use BLEU or ROUGE on model output text for that. A \"good\" loss threshold depends on tokenisation and task, so compare against a baseline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0272","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-062-14-4-experiments-rank-batch-size-demo-2","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s046","q":"How would you design an experiment to compare LoRA ranks and batch sizes?","a":"<p>Use a grid (for example ranks 1, 4, 16 and batch sizes 8, 64, 128), start every run from a freshly loaded base model with the same seed, keep all other settings fixed, train for the same number of epochs, and record validation loss, a task metric, time and memory for each run. Choose the cheapest setting within noise of the best. If runs are expensive, use random search or fewer values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0273","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-062-14-4-experiments-rank-batch-size-demo-2","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s046","q":"What is the bug in this experiment loop?","a":"<p>The loop variable <code>rank</code> is never passed to the layer, so every run uses the layer's default rank and only the batch size really changes. Pass <code>LoRALayer(dense, rank=rank)</code>. Check that each experiment setting is visible in the model, for example by printing the parameter count per run.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for rank in ranks:\n    for batch_size in batch_sizes:\n        ...\n        model.layers[3] = LoRALayer(model.get_layer(&#x27;lm_head&#x27;))\n        ...</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0274","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-062-14-4-experiments-rank-batch-size-demo-2","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s046","q":"How do you count trainable and non-trainable parameters in a Keras model?","a":"<p>Sum the number of elements in each weight tensor: the product of its shape dimensions. <code>model.trainable_weights</code> and <code>model.non_trainable_weights</code> are the two lists, and <code>model.count_params()</code> gives the total. <code>model.summary()</code> prints the same numbers.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>trainable_params = np.sum([np.prod(v.get_shape().as_list()) for v in model.trainable_weights])\nnon_trainable_params = np.sum([np.prod(v.get_shape().as_list()) for v in model.non_trainable_weights])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0275","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-063-14-4-experiments-rank-batch-size-demo-2","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s046","q":"What should you deploy after training a LoRA adapter?","a":"<p>Only the adapter weights (small file) plus the reference to the exact base model and version they were trained for. At inference load the base, attach the adapter, or merge it for the lowest latency. Swapping adapters switches tasks without reloading the base.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0276","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-064-14-5-challenge-flan-t5-small-lora-chapte","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s047","q":"Why develop and debug a fine-tuning pipeline on a small model first?","a":"<p>A small model (FLAN-T5-small, around 77 million parameters) trains in minutes, so data bugs, shape errors and metric code surface quickly and cheaply. Once the pipeline works, change the checkpoint name to a larger model and rerun, expecting better quality at higher cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0277","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-064-14-5-challenge-flan-t5-small-lora-chapte","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s047","q":"Why does the language-model head account for a larger share of parameters in a small model?","a":"<p>Its size is hidden size x vocabulary size, which does not shrink as much as the transformer layers do. For a vocabulary of about 32,000 tokens, a hidden size of 512 gives about 16 million weights, which is a big fraction of a 77-million-parameter model, while for larger models the layers dominate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0278","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-065-15-chapter-5-exercises","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s048","q":"When you apply the same fine-tuning recipe to sentiment analysis, summarisation and question answering, what changes in each case?","a":"<p>The architecture or head (encoder-only with a classification head for sentiment; encoder-decoder or decoder-only for generation), the data format (labels vs target text, task prefix), the input length (long for articles), the loss (cross-entropy over classes or over tokens) and the metric (accuracy or F1, ROUGE, exact match or F1 for QA). The freezing and learning-rate logic stays the same.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0279","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-065-15-chapter-5-exercises","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s048","q":"Which model architecture fits sentiment analysis, summarisation and extractive question answering, and why?","a":"<p>Sentiment: encoder-only (BERT, DistilBERT) with a classification head, because it needs understanding and one label. Summarisation: encoder-decoder (T5, BART) or decoder-only, because it generates new text. Extractive QA: encoder-only with a span-prediction head. Generative QA: encoder-decoder or decoder-only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0280","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-066-15-1-summarisation-with-lora-chapter5-so","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s049","q":"Why does summarisation need a much larger input <code>max_length</code> than translating sentences, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>News articles are long, and truncation cuts away the end, so key information may be lost. A larger length keeps more, but memory and compute grow with sequence length (attention is quadratic), so batches must be smaller. For longer documents use chunking, a long-context model, or summarise in stages.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model_inputs = tokenizer(inputs, max_length=512, truncation=True, padding=&#x27;max_length&#x27;, return_tensors=&#x27;tf&#x27;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0281","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-066-15-1-summarisation-with-lora-chapter5-so","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s049","q":"What is the role of the \"Summarize:\" prefix in FLAN-T5 summarisation, and does fine-tuning still need it?","a":"<p>The prefix names the task for a text-to-text model. FLAN-T5 was instruction-tuned and understands it, and fine-tuning on the same prefix ties it to your data. Keep the prefix the same in training and inference, or the model may not behave as trained.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0282","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-066-15-1-summarisation-with-lora-chapter5-so","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s049","q":"What is the risk in calling <code>load_metric(\"rouge\", trust_remote_code=True)</code>?","a":"<p><code>trust_remote_code=True</code> allows the library to download and run Python code from the Hub on your machine. That is a supply-chain risk, so use it only when you trust the repository, and pin the revision. The <code>evaluate</code> library's <code>evaluate.load(\"rouge\")</code> is the current way to load the metric.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0283","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-067-15-2-sentiment-analysis-transfer-learnin","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s050","q":"What does freezing <code>model.layers[0]</code> do in this DistilBERT classifier?","a":"<p>It freezes the transformer body, so only the classification head is trained. That gives fast training and little overfitting on small data, but the features cannot adapt to the task, so accuracy is usually a bit lower than full fine-tuning. Unfreeze the top layers or all layers with a small learning rate if you need more accuracy.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model.layers[0].trainable = False</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0284","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-067-15-2-sentiment-analysis-transfer-learnin","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s050","q":"Why does <code>distilbert-base-uncased</code> matter for your input text?","a":"<p>The uncased tokenizer lowercases text and strips accents, so capitalisation information is lost. That is fine for sentiment in general, but not when case carries meaning (named entities, for example). Use the cased variant in that case. The tokenizer must match the model checkpoint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0285","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-068-15-3-question-answering-flan-t5-on-squad","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s051","q":"What is the difference between extractive and generative question answering?","a":"<p>Extractive QA predicts the start and end positions of an answer span inside the given context, using an encoder with a span head (BERT-style). Generative QA produces the answer as new text with a seq2seq or decoder-only model, so it can rephrase, combine facts or answer without a span, but it can also hallucinate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0286","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-068-15-3-question-answering-flan-t5-on-squad","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s051","q":"What is special about SQuAD v2 compared with SQuAD v1, and how does the training data handle it?","a":"<p>SQuAD v2 adds questions that cannot be answered from the passage, so the model must also know when to abstain. Those items have an empty answer list, and this code maps them to an empty target string. The standard evaluation reports exact match and F1 on answerable and unanswerable questions separately.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>targets = [answer[&#x27;text&#x27;][0] if len(answer[&#x27;text&#x27;]) &gt; 0 else &quot;&quot; for answer in examples[&quot;answers&quot;]]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0287","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-068-15-3-question-answering-flan-t5-on-squad","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s051","q":"What can go wrong when the model input is built as context followed by the question, with truncation on?","a":"<p>Truncation removes tokens from the end of the string, so for a long passage the question is cut off and the model has nothing to answer. Put the question first (<code>question: ... context: ...</code>), or truncate only the context (a tokenizer with two sequences and <code>truncation=\"only_second\"</code>), or split long contexts into windows.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inputs = [context + &quot; question: &quot; + question for question, context in zip(examples[&quot;question&quot;], examples[&quot;context&quot;])]\nmodel_inputs = tokenizer(inputs, max_length=512, truncation=True, padding=&#x27;max_length&#x27;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0288","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-068-15-3-question-answering-flan-t5-on-squad","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s051","q":"Why is DataCollatorForSeq2Seq used with <code>to_tf_dataset</code>?","a":"<p>The collator pads each batch to its longest sequence (dynamic padding), so short batches do not carry wasted padding. If you pass the model, it also creates <code>decoder_input_ids</code> from the labels. With <code>model=None</code> it does not create them, so the model must build them itself from the labels.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>data_collator = DataCollatorForSeq2Seq(tokenizer, model=None)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0289","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-069-15-3-question-answering-flan-t5-on-squad","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s051","q":"Which metrics suit question answering and why is ROUGE only a rough one?","a":"<p>The standard SQuAD metrics are exact match (the prediction equals a gold answer after normalisation) and token-level F1. ROUGE gives partial credit for overlapping words and is a rough proxy for short answers. For generative QA also assess faithfulness to the context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0290","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-069-15-3-question-answering-flan-t5-on-squad","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s051","q":"What is the bug in this evaluation code?","a":"<p><code>answer = answer(batch)</code> rebinds the name <code>answer</code> from the function to its result (a string). The next run of the cell fails with \"str object is not callable\". Use distinct names for functions and results.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def answer(inputs):\n    ...\n\nbatch = next(iter(validation_dataset))\nanswer = answer(batch)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0291","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-069-15-3-question-answering-flan-t5-on-squad","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s051","q":"How would you check that a QA model handles unanswerable questions properly?","a":"<p>Evaluate separately on answerable and unanswerable items. Check how often the model returns an empty or \"no answer\" output for unanswerable ones, and how often it wrongly abstains on answerable ones. If it always answers, add more unanswerable training examples or calibrate a confidence threshold.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0292","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-070-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"How would you design a chatbot that uses a sentiment model, a summariser, a question answerer and a general text generator?","a":"<p>Put a router in front: it detects the intent of each message and calls the right model. Load every model once at startup, keep conversation state per session, return JSON from a small HTTP API, and add timeouts, logging and error handling. A keyword router is a start; an intent classifier or an LLM router is more robust.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0293","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-070-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"What are the weaknesses of routing by keywords such as \"summarize\" or a question mark?","a":"<p>Messages containing \"summarize\" in another sense, rhetorical questions, or questions without \"?\" are misrouted. Keywords break across languages and phrasing. Use a small intent classifier, or an LLM that picks the tool, and fall back to a default route when confidence is low.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0294","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-070-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"Why would loading FLAN-T5-large twice, once for summarisation and once for QA, be wasteful?","a":"<p>Both are the same checkpoint, so you keep two copies of the weights in memory for no benefit. A text-to-text model chooses the task from the prompt, so one model object can do both with different prefixes. Share the model and tokenizer.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>summarization_model = TFAutoModelForSeq2SeqLM.from_pretrained(&quot;google/flan-t5-large&quot;)\nqa_model = TFAutoModelForSeq2SeqLM.from_pretrained(&quot;google/flan-t5-large&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0295","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-071-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"What is the problem with keeping <code>conversation_history</code> in a module-level global in a Flask app?","a":"<p>The list is shared by all users and requests, so conversations mix, and it is not thread-safe under concurrent requests. With several worker processes each process has its own copy, so state is inconsistent. Store state per session id in Redis or a database.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>app = Flask(__name__)\nconversation_history = []\nconversation_summaries = []</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0296","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-071-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"Why use <code>host='0.0.0.0'</code> with care, and what is the safer default?","a":"<p><code>0.0.0.0</code> listens on all network interfaces, so anyone who can reach the machine can call the API, and this endpoint has no authentication. Bind to <code>127.0.0.1</code> for local use, or put the app behind a reverse proxy with authentication and TLS.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>app.run(host=&#x27;0.0.0.0&#x27;, port=5000)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0297","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-071-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"Why is <code>app.run</code> not the way to run a Flask app in production?","a":"<p>It starts Flask's built-in development server, which is single-purpose, not hardened and not built for concurrency. In production use a WSGI server such as Gunicorn or uWSGI behind a reverse proxy (Nginx), with several workers sized for model memory. Each worker loads its own models, so memory per worker matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0298","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-071-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"What does <code>request.json.get('message')</code> do if the client sends no message, and how would you handle it?","a":"<p>It returns <code>None</code>, which then flows into the history and the model (and <code>.lower()</code> on it raises an error). Validate input at the API boundary: check that the body is JSON, that <code>message</code> is a non-empty string within a length limit, and return a 400 response with an error message otherwise.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>user_message = request.json.get(&#x27;message&#x27;)\nconversation_history.append(f&#x27;User: {user_message}&#x27;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0299","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-071-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"What does the <code>@app.route('/reset', methods=['POST'])</code> decorator do, and why POST?","a":"<p>It registers the function as the handler for that URL and HTTP method. A reset changes server state, so it should use POST (not GET, which must be safe and repeatable, and can be triggered by prefetching or links).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0300","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-071-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"Why is it a problem that the text-generation pipeline's output includes the prompt, and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>By default a Hugging Face <code>text-generation</code> pipeline returns the prompt followed by the continuation, so the user sees their own message (and any added instruction) repeated. Pass <code>return_full_text=False</code> to get only the new text, and set <code>max_new_tokens</code> to limit the length.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response = pipe(prompt)[0][&#x27;generated_text&#x27;]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0301","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-072-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"Why does the sentiment function compare <code>sentiment_score[0][1]</code> with 0.5 after a softmax?","a":"<p>The logits are turned into probabilities that sum to one, and index 1 is the positive class in this SST-2 model (check <code>model.config.id2label</code>). A value over 0.5 means positive is more probable. Always read the label mapping from the config rather than assuming it, and consider a neutral band for low confidence.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sentiment_score = tf.nn.softmax(outputs.logits, axis=-1).numpy()\nsentiment = &quot;positive&quot; if sentiment_score[0][1] &gt; 0.5 else &quot;negative&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0302","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-072-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"Why prefixing a base GPT-2 prompt with \"The user is angry.\" is unlikely to work well, and what would you use instead?","a":"<p>GPT-2 is a plain language model, not instruction-tuned, so it continues the text rather than following the hint, and the output may even echo it. Use an instruction-tuned or chat model with a system prompt that sets tone, or choose templates for negative sentiment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0303","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-072-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"How do you keep a multi-turn chatbot coherent when the model has a limited context window?","a":"<p>Pass the recent turns in the prompt, and summarise or drop older ones when the token budget is exceeded. Keep a running summary of the conversation, keep the system instructions, and retrieve relevant older facts when needed. A history that is stored but never put into the prompt gives the model no memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0304","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-072-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"How would you call and test the chat API from a notebook or script?","a":"<p>Send an HTTP POST with a JSON body using <code>requests</code>. The <code>json=</code> argument serialises the dictionary and sets the content type. Call <code>.json()</code> on the response to parse it, and check <code>response.status_code</code> first. For repeatable tests use Flask's <code>app.test_client()</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from requests import post\nresponse = post(&quot;http://localhost:5000/chat&quot;, json={&#x27;message&#x27;: &#x27;What is the capital of France?&#x27;}).json()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0305","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-072-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"A Flask service hosts a sentiment classifier, a summariser, a QA model and a text generator. How would you serve it at scale?","a":"<p>Move each model behind its own inference service (or a model server) on GPU, so it can scale independently. Use batching, request queues with timeouts, caching of repeated requests, and asynchronous workers for long generations. Keep the web layer thin and stateless with state in an external store, and monitor latency and errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0306","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-072-16-multi-model-chatbot-with-flask","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s052","q":"What does the <code>%%writefile chatbot.py</code> cell magic do in a Jupyter notebook?","a":"<p>It writes the contents of the cell to the file <code>chatbot.py</code> instead of executing it. It lets a notebook create a script, which you then run as a separate process (for example <code>python chatbot.py</code> in the background), so the server does not block the notebook.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0307","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-073-17-1-what-the-course-covered","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s053","q":"How would you decide between prompting, RAG, LoRA fine-tuning and full fine-tuning for a new LLM use case?","a":"<p>Start with prompting (and few-shot examples): cheapest and fastest. Add retrieval (RAG) when the model needs fresh or private knowledge that changes. Use LoRA when you need a consistent style, format or domain behaviour that prompting cannot give reliably, and you have labelled examples. Use full fine-tuning only with large data, a large domain shift and enough compute. Evaluate each step on a task test set before moving up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0308","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-073-17-1-what-the-course-covered","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s053","q":"What business areas benefit from fine-tuned language models, and what extra care do they need?","a":"<p>Healthcare (clinical text), finance (documents, risk) and customer service (support, sentiment) are common. They need privacy controls for personal data, domain evaluation on held-out cases, bias checks, monitoring after launch, and human review for high-stakes decisions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0309","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-074-17-2-advanced-topics-and-trends","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s054","q":"What is federated learning and what are its trade-offs?","a":"<p>Training happens on each device or organisation's local data, and only model updates (gradients or weights) are sent to a server and aggregated (for example by federated averaging). The raw data stays local, which helps privacy rules and on-device learning. Trade-offs: communication cost, non-identical data across clients (which hurts convergence), device heterogeneity, and the fact that updates can still leak information, so techniques such as differential privacy and secure aggregation are added.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0310","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-074-17-2-advanced-topics-and-trends","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s054","q":"What is retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) and how does it differ from fine-tuning?","a":"<p>RAG retrieves relevant documents at query time (embedding search over a vector store) and puts them in the prompt, so the model answers from current external knowledge and can cite it. Fine-tuning changes the model's weights to learn behaviour or style. Use RAG for facts that change or are private, fine-tuning for behaviour, and combine them when both are needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0311","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-074-17-2-advanced-topics-and-trends","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s054","q":"What is a mixture-of-experts (MoE) model and why is it used?","a":"<p>An MoE replaces a dense feed-forward block with many expert networks and a router that sends each token to a few (often top-2) experts. The total parameter count is large, but only a fraction is active per token, so compute per token is much lower than for a dense model of the same size. Trade-offs: all experts need memory, routing and load balancing are hard, and serving is more complex.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0312","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-074-17-2-advanced-topics-and-trends","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s054","q":"What are pruning, quantisation and knowledge distillation, and when would you use each?","a":"<p>Pruning removes weights, neurons or heads that matter little (structured pruning gives real speedups). Quantisation lowers numeric precision (for example 32-bit to 8-bit or 4-bit weights) to cut memory and latency. Distillation trains a smaller student to imitate a larger teacher. Use quantisation first because it is cheap, distillation when you need a fundamentally smaller model, and pruning when hardware benefits from the sparsity. Always re-check accuracy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0313","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-074-17-2-advanced-topics-and-trends","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s054","q":"What is QLoRA and why is it useful?","a":"<p>QLoRA loads the frozen base model in 4-bit precision (NF4 quantisation) and trains LoRA adapters in higher precision on top of it, using paged optimisers to handle memory spikes. It cuts the memory needed to fine-tune large models by several times, so a model with tens of billions of parameters can be tuned on a single GPU, with quality close to 16-bit LoRA.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0314","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-074-17-2-advanced-topics-and-trends","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s054","q":"What is Constitutional AI?","a":"<p>A method from Anthropic to align models using a written set of principles (a \"constitution\"). The model critiques and revises its own answers against those principles, and the revised answers (and AI preference judgements) are used to train it, reducing the amount of human labelling for harmlessness. It is an alignment technique, not an architecture.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"fine-tuning-llm-f0315","card":"q-fine-tuning-llm-074-17-2-advanced-topics-and-trends","sec":"fine-tuning-llm-s054","q":"How does a multimodal model combine text, images and audio?","a":"<p>Each modality has an encoder that turns raw input into feature vectors, and a fusion step combines them (concatenation, cross-attention or projecting image features into the language model's token space), after which one model reasons over the joint representation. Pre-training with paired data (such as image and caption) aligns the modalities.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def multimodal_inference(text_input, image_input, audio_input):\n    text_features = text_encoder(text_input)\n    image_features = image_encoder(image_input)\n    audio_features = audio_encoder(audio_input)\n    combined_features = fusion_layer([text_features, image_features, audio_features])\n    return multimodal_model(combined_features)</code></pre></div>"}];
